  "raw": {
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3861": {
      "title": "Position Description",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Position Description Curriculum Specialist, Indigenous Learning Job Code 3861 Department Centre for Learning and Teaching Employment Type Temporary/Contract Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we live, work and create. OCAD University is Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, internationally recognized for creativity, innovation, and interdisciplinary learning. As the University advances its Academic and Strategic Plan, we are continuing to stengthen our commitment to Indigenous learning, decolonization, and meaningful institutional change. The Opportunity OCAD University is seeking a Curriculum Specialist, Indigenous Learning to lead strategic initiatives that advance Indigenous teaching, learning, and student success across the University. Working within the Centre for Learning & Teaching (CLT) and reporting to the Manager, Teaching Support & Curriculum Enhancement, the Curriculum Specialist collaborates closely with the Director, Indigenous Initiatives, and a broad network of Indigenous and academic partners to strengthen Indigenous curriculum development and renewal across undergraduate and graduate programs. Through curriculum review, faculty engagement, online course development, and the renewal of OCAD U's Indigenous Learning Outcomes (ILOs), the successful candidate will help advance Indigenous histories, perspectives, knowledges, methodologies, and cultural art and design practices throughout the curriculum. As a member of CLT, the Curriculum Specialist will join a collaborative team of teaching and learning professionals dedicated to supporting faculty, students, Teaching Assistants, and instructional staff. Working in consultation with the Chair of the Indigenous Visual Culture (INVC) program, the Indigenous Student Centre (ISC), the Indigenous Education Council (IEC), and other campus and community partners, the successful candidate will play a key role in supporting OCAD U's commitment to Indigenous teaching and learning and the priorities of the University's Academic and Strategic Plan . What You’ll Do Advance Indigenous Curriculum and Student Success Lead and support curriculum development and renewal across OCAD U’s undergraduate and graduate programs, strengthening engagement with Indigenous histories, perspectives, knowledges, methodologies, and cultural practices in art and design Collaborate with the Indigenous Student Centre and Indigenous Visual Culture program to assess Indigenous students’ learning experiences and identify and address systemic barriers to learning Develop teaching and learning resources and faculty professional learning initiatives that support Indigenous student experiences and success Provide subject matter expertise in Indigenous pedagogies, methodologies, and decolonizing approaches to strengthen the Centre for Learning & Teaching’s programming and engagement Lead the Renewal of Indigenous Learning Outcomes (ILO) Organize and lead the redesign and renewal of OCAD U’s ILO Develop strategies, resources, documentation, and processes that support ongoing engagement with the ILO across the University Coordinate and conduct consultations with faculty, the Indigenous Education Council, the Indigenous Cultural Advisory Circle, and Indigenous community partners, including Six Nations of the Grand River and the Mississaugas of the Credit First Nation Develop Learning and Community-Engagement Initiatives Co-facilitate and support the design, development, and production of an online Indigenous art, design, and history course in collaboration with a multidisciplinary course-development team Develop and deliver community-engagement programming that strengthens faculty and staff understanding of Indigenous learning and decolonization Facilitate professional learning and complex conversations to build institutional capacity and support meaningful organizational change What You Bring Indigenous Citizenship, Knowledge and Community Relationships Indigenous citizenship (First Nations, Inuit, Métis), connection to an Indigenous community, a history and/or evidence of lived experience and an understanding of Indigenous culture(s) and practices In depth knowledge of Indigenous pedagogies, methodologies and ways of knowing at the post-secondary level, including decolonizing and Indigenizing pedagogies, land-based learning, storytelling, experiential learning, and intergenerational and relational approaches to teaching and learning Significant experience living and/or working with Indigenous communities and well-versed in protocol matters related to Indigenous relationships is essential Demonstrated intercultural competence; experience working with diverse Indigenous communities, including Elders, Knowledge Keepers, community members, faculty, academic administrators, administrative staff, program staff and students Understanding of the barriers and learning needs of Indigenous post-secondary students Education and Experience Successful completion of a graduate/Master's degree with a specialization in Indigenous Education, Curriculum Studies or similar, Ph.D./Ed.D preferred Four (4) years of progressive experience working in Indigenous educational programming, community engagement, or related fields, or an equivalent combination of education, community-based knowledge, and lived experience. Three (3) years of experience developing collaborative partnerships to advance decolonization, including experience of organizational change-based projects in comparable sectors; demonstrated experience facilitating professional learning to build institutional capacity Familiarity with post-secondary studies in art and design is essential; background in studio art or design a strong asset Post-secondary teaching experience or experience in a post-secondary teaching centre, writing centre or similar academic support unit is an asset. An equivalent combination of teaching, community, and/or professional experience demonstrating the required knowledge and competencies will also be considered. Curriculum, Consultation and Program Leadership Experience in the development and delivery of hybrid and online curriculum, an asset Demonstrated ability to manage programming with proven vision and initiative grounded in Indigenous epistemologies Program planning skills with ability to meet deadlines and manage complex projects Experience designing and implementing consultation and needs assessment processes Collaboration and Communication Facilitation and consensus-building skills, with demonstrated ability to navigate complex and sensitive conversations across diverse community members, with faculty and staff Strong collaborative work orientation; ability to work both independently and consultatively within a team environment Exceptional interpersonal and communication skills, with demonstrated ability to build collaborative relationships, anticipate and respond to resistance and influence curriculum development and educational initiatives through dialogue, consultation and partnership Excellent oral and written communication skills including academic and professional writing experience Commitment and Flexibility Demonstrated commitment to the principles and practices of equity, anti-racism, decolonizing and accessibility; experience promoting a respectful work and learning environment for students, faculty and staff Flexibility to adjust hours and location of work as needed to support programs and events outside of regular office hours and location Work Arrangement & Compensation Compensation: Hiring range: $46.33 to $53.11 per hour. Salary range: $46.33 to $60.88 per hour Hours of Work: Monday to Friday, for a total of 35 hours per week. This is a temporary contract expected to continue until May 31, 2027. Mode of Work: This position is eligible for hybrid work arrangements; however, a minimum of three (3) days per week of on campus work is required with flexibility to adjust the days and times of work on campus to meet programming needs. The review of applications will begin August 31st and continue until the position is filled. Why Work at OCAD University A vibrant and creative work environment surrounded by artists, designers, educators, and researchers Access to employee assistance program Paid time off during the annual Winter Closure A centrally located downtown campus with easy access to public transit The opportunity to work collaboratively with Indigenous faculty, staff, students, leaders, and community partners A meaningful role supporting Indigenous student success and advancing curricular and institutional change If you are an Indigenous applicant and require support during the recruitment process, please reach out to Olivia Bowes, Talent Acquisition Coordinator at oliviabowes@ocadu.ca . Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3868": {
      "title": "Position Description",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Position Description Executive Director, Strategic Revenue Initiatives Job Code 3868 Department Office of the Vice-President, Finance & Administration Employment Type Temporary/Contract Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we live, work and create OCAD University , Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, is a vibrant community of bold, curious and compassionate artists, designers and scholars who are imagining and creating a joyful, equitable and sustainable world. The University is an internationally renowned hub for art, design, digital media, research, innovation and creativity, and arts administration. It embraces collaborative and interdisciplinary approaches to change-making through art, design-thinking curriculum and research, making OCAD U a local, regional, national and global leader in art and design. It is an exciting time to join the OCAD U community as the University is at a key stage in its institutional transformation, with the implementation of its Academic and Strategic Plan and guiding principles — driving positive impact; decolonization, indigenization and equity; environmental sustainability; emerging as a vibrant hub; student centered approaches; innovation in learning teaching and research; and financial stewardship. The Executive Director, Strategic Revenue Initiatives provides strategic leadership for the identification, development, and advancement of new revenue-generating opportunities that support OCAD University’s long-term financial sustainability, institutional priorities, brand and community values. Reporting to the Vice-President, Finance & Administration (VPFA), the Executive Director is responsible for developing and implementing a comprehensive revenue diversification strategy focused on expanding existing revenue streams, identifying emerging business opportunities, and establishing new commercial ventures, partnerships and enterprise initiatives. The role works collaboratively across the University, including, but not limited to: the School of Continuing Studies, Copy & Print Services, Merchandise & Design Product Sales and Event Sales. The role operates at a strategic level, assessing market opportunities, developing business cases driving business performance, and identifying new opportunities for revenue growth. The Executive Director serves as a central catalyst for revenue growth and business development across the institution. While the role does not assume day-to-day operational responsibility for existing units or override the authority of leaders currently accountable for the various portfolios, the Executive Director is expected to work alongside established unit leaders to enhance revenue generation, operational sustainability, market reach and institutional growth. Success in this role requires the ability to influence, align, and mobilize cross-functional teams within this shared accountability framework to advance integrated revenue growth strategies. This is one (1) year appointment with the possibility of renewal dependent on the metrics of success and growth. Within six (6) months of the term, the incumbent will be expected to develop and present a strategic plan for the University that allows for revenue growth within the associated departments listed above. Summary of Responsibilities: Strategic Leadership & Revenue Growth Develop and implement a comprehensive auxiliary revenue diversification strategy aligned with OCAD U’s strategic, academic, financial and operational priorities. Lead the development  of formal business plans, feasibility studies, financial models and implementation plans for new revenue initiatives. Identify and evaluate new revenue-generating opportunities, partnerships, and commercial ventures, including market expansion and innovation initiatives. Ensure auxiliary operations contribute meaningfully to institutional financial sustainability targets. Enterprise Growth Initiatives In collaboration with members of the Executive Team, lead cross-functional initiatives designed to generate new sources of institutional revenue Establish and monitor key performance indicators (KPIs), financial targets, and benchmarking measures aligned with sector best practices. Collaborate with leaders to identify new opportunities for professional learning, auxiliary services, facility use, and partnership development. Facilitate the exploration and development of new enterprises and revenue-generating services. Drive continuous improvement, innovation, and cost optimization across auxiliary functions. Support existing unit leaders in assessing opportunities for revenue expansion, while respecting their operational accountability and decision-making authority within their portfolios. Develop frameworks to help prioritize revenue opportunities based on strategic alignment, financial viability, market demand, operational capacity, risk and institutional impact. Financial Management & Business Planning Develop financial projections, business models, revenue forecasts, and return on investment analyses for proposed revenue initiatives. Ensure rigorous financial analysis, performance tracking, and reporting frameworks are in place to support decision-making. Monitor and evaluate the performance of approved revenue diversification initiatives against established targets, timelines and key performance indicators. Advise pricing strategies, contract negotiations, and commercial agreements to maximize revenue and mitigate risk in collaboration with unit heads to ensure new business initiatives align with the University’s long-term strategy Support integrated planning across revenue-generating activities to reduce duplication, identify synergies and align initiatives with institutional priorities. Ensure financial analysis and reporting frameworks support transparent, evidence-informed decision-making Governance, Reporting & Accountability Ensure strong governance, compliance, and risk management practices across auxiliary enterprises. Develops implementation plans for approved revenue initiatives and monitors execution progress, reporting regularly to the Vice-President, Finance & Administration on status, outcomes, risks, and corrective actions required Provide no less frequent than semi-annual updates to the Audit, Finance and Risk Committee (AFRC) on operations, revenue metrics, and KPI achievement. Prepare and present strategic reports and recommendations to senior leadership and governing bodies. Ensure transparency and alignment with institutional policies, regulatory requirements, and public-sector accountability expectations. Stakeholder Engagement & Partnerships Build and maintain strong relationships with internal stakeholders, including Deans, administrative leaders, and service units. Develop and manage external partnerships with vendors, service providers, and commercial collaborators. Represent the University in negotiations with external partners and business development initiatives. People Leadership Lead project teams, working groups, and cross-functional initiatives in support of revenue diversification objectives. Foster a culture of accountability, innovation, collaboration, and service excellence. Provide strategic guidance, business development expertise, and mentorship to colleagues engaged in revenue-generating initiatives. Support change management activities associated with the development and implementation of new revenue opportunities. Qualifications: A graduate degree in Business Administration, Commerce, Finance, or a related field, or an equivalent combination of education and experience with special consideration for the professional and community-based experience of equity-deserving groups Minimum ten (10) years of progressive leadership experience in revenue-generating operations, auxiliary services, commercial enterprises, or related fields. Experience within a post-secondary institution or similarly complex, public-sector environment is strongly preferred, with specific experience and knowledge of Continuing Studies in higher education. Demonstrated success in developing business strategies, growing revenue, and managing multi-unit operations. Experience reporting to senior executives and governance bodies (e.g., Board committees). Strong business acumen, financial management, and strategic planning capabilities. Demonstrated ability to drive revenue growth while balancing mission and public accountability. Expertise in business case development, financial modeling, and scenario planning. Exceptional stakeholder management and influencing skills. Strong communication skills, including executive-level presentation and reporting. Ability to lead change, innovation, and complex organizational initiatives. Ability to both think and act strategically, balancing vision with the ability to implement that vision. A collaborative and consultative working style with an ability to develop and maintain effective working relationships internally and externally in a manner that gains & maintains trust, respect and cooperation. Excellent interpersonal skills and judgement, with a demonstrated ability to deal with sensitive or confidential matters with discretion. Personal qualities that include integrity, openness, fairness, resourcefulness, and confidence, coupled with an accessible style, optimism, enthusiasm and high emotional intelligence Demonstrated commitment to the principles of equity, diversity, inclusion, accessibility, decolonization and Indigenization. Mode of Work: Hybrid Compensation: $138,200.00 to $162,554.00 annually. * The successful incumbent has the option of pay in lieu or enrollment in benefits. Hours of Work: Monday to Friday, 35 hours per week. This is a temporary contract with an expected duration of 1 year. Application Deadline: Interested applicants are invited to submit an updated * resume and cover letter ( PDF ) by selecting \"Apply Now\" below. The review of applications will begin September 21 st 2026 and will continue until the position has been filled. * Please upload your resume and cover letter as one PDF . Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code As an institution committed to art, design, digital media and related scholarship, OCAD University recognizes the profound and essential value that diversity brings to the creation, reception and circulation of creative practices and discourse. OCAD University understands that valuing diverse creative practices and forms of knowledge are essential to and enrich the institution’s core mission and vision as an art and design university with a local and global scope. As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-deserving communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. OCAD University is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCAD U’s Policy of Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities . All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3877": {
      "title": "Position Description",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Position Description Graduate Research Assistant: Rerouting: African Art and/as Infrastructure Job Code 3877 Department Research Labs & Projects Employment Type Temporary/Contract Apply Now Graduate Research Assistant: Rerouting: African Art and/as Infrastructure OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we work, stand and create. OCAD University , Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, is a vibrant community of bold, curious and compassionate artists, designers and scholars who are imagining and creating a joyful, equitable and sustainable world. The University is an internationally renowned hub for art, design, digital media, research, innovation and creativity, and arts administration. It embraces collaborative and interdisciplinary approaches to change-making through art, design-thinking curriculum and research, making OCAD U a local, regional, national and global leader in art and design. It is an exciting time to join the OCAD U community. The University is at a key stage in its institutional transformation, with the implementation of its Academic and Strategic Plan and guiding principles—driving positive impact, decolonization, indigenization, and equity, environmental sustainability, emerging as a vibrant hub, student-centered approaches, innovation in learning, teaching, and research, and financial stewardship. Project Details: Can a power grid be an artistic medium? What about a water pipe? Or a railway? Rerouting: African Art and/as Infrastructure asks what happens when artists treat infrastructure itself as raw material, using it to expose how systems distribute resources unevenly and to imagine how those systems can be assembled differently. Earlier case studies explored creative responses to South Africa’s electricity and water crises; the next stage of the project will turn to public transportation, sanitation, and cellular networks across Southern Africa. The Research Assistant will work closely with Principal Investigator (PI), Dr. Anna Stielau in Fall 2026 to develop the project’s next phase and prepare a SSHRC Insight Development Grant application. Applications are particularly encouraged from MA, MFA, MDes, and other graduate students whose work intersects with contemporary art, media and technology, African studies, infrastructure and the built environment, or postcolonial and decolonial studies. Expertise in African art or history is welcome but not required. The position will provide hands-on experience in developing a major humanities research project from its earliest stages. The successful candidate will gain experience in interdisciplinary research, building literature reviews, evaluating case studies, and the practical mechanics of preparing a competitive grant application in Canada. Responsibilities Working under the supervision of the Principal Investigator, Dr. Stielau, in support of the project's research goals and objectives Researching contemporary Southern African artists, artworks, exhibitions, and institutional collections to identify potential case studies Conducting interdisciplinary literature searches to assist in developing an annotated project bibliography Locating, organizing, and synthesizing relevant scholarship and archival material, and preparing concise research summaries for discussion with the PI Assisting with research related to the project’s SSHRC Insight Development Grant application, including fact-finding around the proposed fieldwork budget Assisting with reference checking, document preparation, and final assembly of grant materials in accordance with SSHRC requirements Participating in the provision of a safe and secure environment for all members of the University community and its facilities Qualifications Current enrollment in a relevant graduate program, with priority given to OCAD University students Strong research, reading, and written communication skills Strong organizational skills and attention to detail Ability to work independently and communicate clearly and consistently about research progress Interest in one or more of the following areas: contemporary art and visual culture; media and technology; infrastructure and the built environment; African or Global South art and media; postcolonial/decolonial studies. Previous experience working with scholarly databases, conducting in-depth research, and using Zotero or other citation-management software is an asset Demonstrated commitment to the principles of equity and diversity and experience promoting a respectful work and learning environment for students, staff and faculty Hours of Work: Approximately 100 hours total over the period of September 2026 to February 2027. Scheduling will be arranged in consultation with the successful candidate. Work Environment: The position will primarily involve remote research, with occasional in-person or Teams meetings with the Principal Investigator at 100 McCaul St, as required. Compensation: $37.28 per hour, plus 6% in lieu of benefits plus vacation pay, as applicable, per the provisions of the Employment Standards Act Please note the following Applicants must be eligible to work in Canada for any employer (with a valid work or study permit, SIN and Canadian bank account). If you are a work or study permit holder, please note a valid Social Insurance Number (SIN) and Canadian bank account is a condition of employment. Current students at OCAD University will be given priority consideration for all Research Assistant positions, with the highest priority given to those not currently holding any other TA/RA positions at the University. Applications should include: A one-page cover letter explaining the candidate’s interest A curriculum vitae The contact information for three (3) referees Please upload all three (3) components as one PDF . Incomplete applications will not be considered. Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code Interested applicants are invited to submit their application in confidence addressed to Dr. Anna Stielau, Assistant Professor of Art History and Visual Culture, Faculty of Arts and Science , online by clicking the “Apply for this Position” button below. Shortlisted candidates may be invited to a brief conversation with the PI to discuss their interests and experience. The review of applications will begin on September 20, 2026 and will continue until the position has been filled. If you require application support, please contact the People & Culture Office. Please note that People & Culture cannot update you on the status of your application as these searches are decentralized. You will only be contacted if you are shortlisted for an interview. If the posting is advertised, we are still accepting applications. As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-seeking communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. We encourage members of designated equity-seeking groups to self-identify within the voluntary Applicant Questionnaire . OCAD U is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCAD U’s Policy of Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities . All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3836": {
      "title": "Position Description",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Position Description Indigenous Student Success Coordinator Job Code 3836 Department Indigenous Student Centre Employment Type Temporary/Contract Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we live, work and create OCAD University , Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, is a vibrant community of bold, curious and compassionate artists, designers and scholars who are imagining and creating a joyful, equitable and sustainable world. The University is an internationally renowned hub for art, design, digital media, research, innovation and creativity, and arts administration. It embraces collaborative and interdisciplinary approaches to change-making through art, design-thinking curriculum and research, making OCAD U a local, regional, national and global leader in art and design. It is an exciting time to join the OCAD U community as the University is at a key stage in its institutional transformation, with the implementation of its Academic and Strategic Plan and guiding principles — driving positive impact; decolonization, indigenization and equity; environmental sustainability; emerging as a vibrant hub; student centered approaches; innovation in learning teaching and research; and financial stewardship. Reporting to the Manager, Indigenous Student Centre (ISC), the Indigenous Student Success Coordinator is responsible for providing Indigenous students with academic support, referrals to student support services both on and off campus, as well as enhancing Indigenous students’ experience through academic and cultural supports, programming, and activities. The Coordinator is responsive to individual student needs, providing support with financial aid, academic advising, and offering advocacy and guidance with academic and non-academic policies. The Coordinator is responsible for planning and implementing services and programs designed to facilitate the academic success of Indigenous students at OCAD University (OCAD U). The Coordinator works to ensure that Indigenous students are supported with culturally relevant services, working closely with Indigenous Student Centre staff, faculty and OCAD U support services to facilitate open, respectful communication to meet the needs of Indigenous students.  Regular on-site attendance is an essential requirement of this position to ensure accessible, responsive, and culturally appropriate support for students. Summary of Responsibilities: Responsible for coordinating, supporting, and enhancing core Indigenous Student Centre weekly programming and workshops aimed at supporting Indigenous students’ social, cultural and emotional wellbeing Collaborate with the Manager, Indigenous Student Centre and ISC team to create new programs that enhance the Indigenous student experience at OCAD University; support and facilitate the development of student-led programming in partnership with Indigenous students and student monitors. Maintain a consistent on-campus presence to provide support and referrals to Indigenous students in distress and as needed; act as a liaison between students and available supports Provide advocacy services and guidance to Indigenous students navigating academic and non-academic policies and processes such as academic misconduct, appeals, and petitions; formally advocate on behalf of Indigenous students and provide support through the relevant policy or process as needed or requested Provide academic advising, support and information to students pertaining to procedures, program requirements, dates and deadlines; work closely with Student Advising, Office of the Registrar and relevant support units Maintain current knowledge and comprehensive understanding of relevant academic and non-academic policies and processes including Student Petitions, Grade Appeals, Non-Academic Misconduct Policy, Response to Violent or Threatening Behaviour, Respectful Work and Learning Environment Policy; to effectively support student concerns and referrals to the relevant policy holder when necessary Maintain proactive communications, including the ISC weekly newsletter, social media accounts and the department’s website, to build awareness, promote events and ensure access to accurate, timely and relevant information; to connect with Indigenous OCAD U students, staff, faculty, alumni, Indigenous communities and partners Provide information and guidance to Indigenous students regarding internal and external bursaries, awards, financial supports; work closely with the Financial Aid & Awards office, providing referrals as needed Maintain knowledge of housing supports available for Indigenous OCAD U students; community programs, resources and housing partners Coordinate Orientation and Welcome events for new students and represent the Indigenous Student Centre at University events. With support from the Manager, Indigenous Student Centre, maintain collaborative relationships with OCAD U’s existing student support services to ensure Indigenous student needs are integrated into service delivery throughout the University; including Student Advising, Office of the Registrar, Student Wellness Centre, Student Accessibility Services, Centre for Learning and Teaching, Financial Aid & Awards, Centre for Emerging Artists & Designers Maintain a resource list for external agencies and organizations that provide supports for Indigenous students, including grants and awards, housing, career opportunities, cultural supports, and health and wellness Track persistence and retention of Indigenous students on an ongoing basis and develop retention plans in collaboration with the Manager, Indigenous Student Centre Supervise the Centre’s Student Monitors including participating in hiring, managing schedules, performance and approving biweekly time entry Serve on internal and external committees as needed Support the Manager, Indigenous Student Centre in other related functions and special projects contributing successful operation of the Indigenous Student Centre Qualifications: Indigenous citizenship (First Nations, Métis, Inuit), connection to an Indigenous community, lived experience and an understanding of the Indigenous culture(s) and practices Experience with the administration of Indigenous community programming, education, and arts Understanding of Indigenous cultural, educational, political, and economic conditions Post-secondary degree or equivalent, with three (3) years of administrative work experience, preferably in a post-secondary educational environment A demonstrated commitment to providing outstanding customer service in a diverse environment Excellent written and oral communication skills, interpersonal skills, and strong self-management skills Demonstrated professionalism, and the ability to exercise tact, good judgment and diplomacy Strong working knowledge of all Microsoft Office applications, and experience working with databases Demonstrated attention to detail, strong organizational and time management skills, and the ability to juggle competing demands, prioritize tasks and meet deadlines Demonstrated ability to take initiative and be an active contributor in the planning, formulation and implementation of student-centered services Proven ability to work independently and participate collaboratively in a team environment Demonstrated commitment to the principles of equity and diversity, and experience promoting a respectful work and learning environment for students, staff and faculty Flexibility in work hours; must be willing and able to work occasional evenings, as required Mode of Work: O n-campus 5 days per week Compensation: Hiring Range - $31.59 to $36.14 per hour commensurate with experience, plus 4% vacation pay and 7% in lieu of benefits; Salary Range - $31.59 to $41.52 per hour. Hours of Work: Monday to Friday, for a total of 35 hours per week. This is a temporary contract expected to continue until May 31, 2027. This posting is for an existing vacancy. Application Deadline: Interested applicants are invited to submit an updated * resume and cover letter ( PDF ) by selecting \"Apply Now\" below. The review of applications will begin August 7th, 2026 and will continue until the position has been filled. * Please upload your resume and cover letter as one PDF . Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code As an institution committed to art, design, digital media and related scholarship, OCAD University recognizes the profound and essential value that diversity brings to the creation, reception and circulation of creative practices and discourse. OCAD University understands that valuing diverse creative practices and forms of knowledge are essential to and enrich the institution’s core mission and vision as an art and design university with a local and global scope. As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-deserving communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. OCAD University is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCAD U’s Policy of Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities . All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3864": {
      "title": "Position Description",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Position Description Lead, Alumni Engagement Job Code 3864 Department Advancement Employment Type Temporary/Contract Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we live, work and create OCAD University , Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, is a vibrant community of bold, curious and compassionate artists, designers and scholars who are imagining and creating a joyful, equitable and sustainable world. The University is an internationally renowned hub for art, design, digital media, research, innovation and creativity, and arts administration. It embraces collaborative and interdisciplinary approaches to change-making through art, design-thinking curriculum and research, making OCAD U a local, regional, national and global leader in art and design. It is an exciting time to join the OCAD U community as the University is at a key stage in its institutional transformation, with the implementation of its Academic and Strategic Plan and guiding principles — driving positive impact; decolonization, indigenization and equity; environmental sustainability; emerging as a vibrant hub; student centered approaches; innovation in learning teaching and research; and financial stewardship. Reporting to the Director of Annual Giving & Alumni Relations and working closely with academic faculties and programs, Marketing & Communications, the RBC Centre for Emerging Artists & Designers, Admissions & Recruitment, Advancement, and other campus partners, the Lead, Alumni Engagement will assess, structure, and expand ways for alumni to be involved, creating a comprehensive roster of engagement opportunities that leverage alumni expertise and strengthen connections between alumni, students, faculty, and the broader OCAD U community. This role will identify and assess existing alumni engagement activities across the institution, establish processes that support coordination and collaboration, and develop new initiatives to address gaps and broaden opportunities for meaningful alumni participation. The position will contribute to building a more connected and engaged alumni community while supporting institutional priorities related to student success, experiential learning, mentorship, recruitment, and lifelong engagement. OCAD University is seeking a Lead, Alumni Engagement to help strengthen and coordinate alumni engagement across the institution. This one-year term position will play a critical role in creating a more centralized and strategic approach to alumni engagement. Summary of Responsibilities: Serve as the University's central point of contact for alumni engagement activities, providing coordination, guidance, and support across faculties, departments, and administrative units. Conduct an inventory of existing alumni engagement initiatives and develop and maintain a centralized roster of opportunities for alumni involvement. Create and implement an annual alumni engagement plan that supports institutional priorities and advances a coordinated, university-wide approach to alumni relations. Collaborate with academic faculties and departments to support alumni engagement objectives and strengthen connections between alumni and academic programs. Partner with the RBC Centre for Emerging Artists & Designers to expand opportunities for alumni involvement in mentorship, experiential learning, career development, and other student-facing initiatives. Work with Recruitment & Admissions to develop meaningful opportunities for alumni participation in student recruitment activities, including ambassador programs, portfolio reviews, outreach initiatives, and prospective student engagement. Identify gaps in existing programming and develop new initiatives that enhance alumni participation and strengthen connections among alumni, students, faculty, and OCAD U. Collaborate with Advancement and Marketing & Communications to promote alumni programs and opportunities through a variety of communication channels. Establish processes for tracking alumni engagement, maintain accurate records in Raiser's Edge and other systems, and monitor participation and outcomes. Engage with alumni to understand their interests, identify opportunities for involvement, and assess their willingness to participate in alumni programs and initiatives. Represent the Alumni Relations team at events by welcoming attendees and fostering meaningful connections with alumni. Evaluate the effectiveness of alumni engagement activities and provide recommendations for continuous improvement and long-term sustainability. Qualifications: Minimum of five (5) years of experience in alumni relations, community engagement, or stakeholder relations Post secondary degree, or equivalent Demonstrated ability to build and maintain effective relationships with a wide range of stakeholders, including alumni, students, faculty, staff, volunteers, and external partners. Experience developing, coordinating, and implementing engagement programs, events, or volunteer initiatives. Strong project management and organizational skills, with the ability to manage multiple priorities and meet deadlines in a collaborative environment. Excellent written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills, with the ability to tailor messaging to diverse audiences. Strong analytical skills and experience collecting, tracking, and interpreting participation and engagement data to inform decision-making and evaluate outcomes. Experience working with constituent relationship management (CRM) systems; familiarity with Raiser's Edge or similar advancement databases is considered an asset. Demonstrated ability to work independently and collaboratively across departments and organizational levels. Knowledge of higher education, alumni relations, advancement, student engagement, or the arts and design sector is an asset. Demonstrated commitment to the principles of equity and diversity, and experience promoting a respectful work and learning environment for students, staff and faculty Mode of Work: Hybrid Compensation: $72,077.00 annually to $84,784.00 annually. * The successful incumbent has the option of pay in lieu or enrollment in benefits. Hours of Work: Monday to Friday, 35 hours per week. This is a temporary contract with an expected duration of 1 year. Application Deadline: Interested applicants are invited to submit an updated * resume and cover letter ( PDF ) by selecting \"Apply Now\" below. The review of applications will begin Tuesday August 25 th and will continue until the position has been filled. * Please upload your resume and cover letter as one PDF . Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code As an institution committed to art, design, digital media and related scholarship, OCAD University recognizes the profound and essential value that diversity brings to the creation, reception and circulation of creative practices and discourse. OCAD University understands that valuing diverse creative practices and forms of knowledge are essential to and enrich the institution’s core mission and vision as an art and design university with a local and global scope. As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-deserving communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. OCAD University is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCAD U’s Policy of Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities . All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3876": {
      "title": "Position Description",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Position Description Program Development Specialist Job Code 3876 Department Admissions & Recruitment Employment Type Permanent Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we live, work and create OCAD University , Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, is a vibrant community of bold, curious and compassionate artists, designers and scholars who are imagining and creating a joyful, equitable and sustainable world. The University is an internationally renowned hub for art, design, digital media, research, innovation and creativity, and arts administration. It embraces collaborative and interdisciplinary approaches to change-making through art, design-thinking curriculum and research, making OCAD U a local, regional, national and global leader in art and design. It is an exciting time to join the OCAD U community as the University is at a key stage in its institutional transformation, with the implementation of its Academic and Strategic Plan and guiding principles — driving positive impact; decolonization, indigenization and equity; environmental sustainability; emerging as a vibrant hub; student centered approaches; innovation in learning teaching and research; and financial stewardship. Reporting to the Manager, Business Strategy, the Program Development Specialist develops non-credit learning opportunities that highlight the value and increase the profile of OCAD University’s (OCAD U) community of artists and designers. The incumbent supports the development of market-driven, innovative non-credit learning opportunities in art & design that meet the aspirations of learners and emerging creatives, while enhancing OCAD U’s local, national and global impact. This role emphasizes the engagement of alumni, industry and community partners in program development and special projects and works within a business development context to ensure programs are sustainable and return value to OCAD U. Summary of Responsibilities : Program Development, Research, and Admin Collaborate closely with faculty, industry professionals, and stakeholders to develop specialized non-credit programs and courses tailored to the needs and aspirations of mid-career, emerging professionals in art and design and other potential learner segments that hold strategic value to OCAD U Work closely with international partners to develop and deliver short courses, workshops and residencies Support the delivery of existing non-credit programs, including supporting incoming participants with registrations, drafting and distributing relevant communications, and general support, through the CRM and Registrations systems Work closely with the Manager to ensure programs are financially sustainable and are relevant to new and emerging markets Lead the development and submission of grant applications and other external funding opportunities, serving as the primary point of contact and consulting with the Manager as required Assess and advise on the viability and feasibility of new learning opportunities as consulted Prepare budget proposals, monitor expenditures, and ensure fiscal responsibility in accordance with university policies; provide administrative support in managing program budgets, financial resources, and procurement processes Recruit, onboard, and support a diverse team of instructors and industry experts who bring practical experience and specialized knowledge to the classroom; ensure programs integrate opportunities for alumni engagement Provide insights, data, and reports to inform strategic marketing, branding, and advertising opportunities Work closely with the Manager and Director, Learning Pathways, to research, identify and propose networks, partnerships and relationships that enhance the relevance of art & design education for lifelong learners and workforce development across various sectors Develop and advance new and existing learner-centred frameworks and design thinking principles to invite and develop emerging, market-relevant learning opportunities. Work with campus stakeholders (e.g., School of Continuing Studies, Admissions, Recruitment) to map program proposals against other offerings, policies, and procedures to ensure they are distinct, have a clear value proposition, and are viable Work closely with Marketing & Communications to create communication and marketing materials that are relevant to learners, promote enrolment in special programs, increase program visibility, and attract prospective learners, as needed Relationship Development Cultivate relationships with alumni to encourage their participation in non-credit program development, advisory activities, and guest lectures Collaborate with the Alumni Relations team to ensure alumni relationships are cultivated at an institutional level Support the development and implementation of the strategic development plan by coordinating and facilitating engagement activities with key partners through events, public engagements, workshops, and co-design sessions Student-Centred Advising and Support Provide support and guidance to learners for program selection, application, and registration as needed, including 1:1 advising, presentations and review of supplemental application material (ie. portfolios, written statements). Work with the Manager to determine the admissibility and readiness of learners to enrol in application-based programs. Contribute to the development of diversity, equity, inclusivity, and accessibility (DEIA) strategies throughout all programs, services and communications Support marketing and recruitment events, activities and initiatives as needed, which may include occasional weekend and evening hours. Support the Manager and Director in other related projects that contribute to the successful operation of the Admissions, Recruitment and Continuing Studies departments. Qualifications : Master’s degree in Art, Design, Education, or a related field Four (4) years of experience in program management, preferably in adult learning environments Demonstrated expertise in developing and delivering educational programs tailored to mid-career professionals Demonstrated research and analytical skills, including the ability to assess market trends, learner needs, and emerging opportunities Experience with the LMS; experience with the CRM and registration system is required Strong understanding of curriculum development, instructional design, and educational best practices Exceptional leadership, communication, and organizational skills Proven ability to collaborate effectively with diverse stakeholders and build strong partnerships Experience developing and sustaining strategic partnerships that support program development, learner engagement, and organizational objectives Commitment to fostering a supportive and inclusive learning environment Commitment to support research-driven projects in cross functional teams Demonstrated commitment to the principles of equity and diversity and experience promoting a respectful work and learning environment for student, staff, and faculty Mode of Work: On campus, 3 days per week. This position is eligible for hybrid work arrangements; however, a minimum of four days per week of on-campus work is required, with flexibility to adjust on-campus days and hours to meet department needs. Occasional evening and weekend work will be required, with shift premiums applied in accordance with the Collective Agreement. All flexibility and scheduling provisions are subject to the terms outlined in the Collective Agreement. Compensation: Hiring Range - $66,925.74 to $76,724.74 per annum, commensurate with experience, plus benefits; Salary Range - $66,925.74 to $87,958.44 per annum. Hours of Work : Monday to Friday, 35 hours per week. This posting is for an existing position. Application Deadline: Interested applicants are invited to submit an updated * resume and cover letter ( PDF )by selecting \"Apply Now\" below. Review of applicants will begin September 11, 2026. * Please upload your resume and cover letter as one PDF . Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-deserving communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. OCAD University is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCAD U’s Policy of Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3858": {
      "title": "Position Description",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Position Description Sessional Instructor: Foresight Studio, School of Graduate Studies, Fall/Winter 2026-27 Job Code 3858 Department Graduate Studies Employment Type Temporary/Contract Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we live, work and create OCAD University , Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, is a vibrant community of bold, curious and compassionate artists, designers and scholars who are imagining and creating a joyful, equitable and sustainable world. The University is an internationally renowned hub for art, design, digital media, research, innovation and creativity, and arts administration. It embraces collaborative and interdisciplinary approaches to change-making through art, design-thinking curriculum and research, making OCAD U a local, regional, national and global leader in art and design. It is an exciting time to join the OCAD U community as the University is at a key stage in its institutional transformation, with the implementation of its Academic and Strategic Plan and guiding principles — driving positive impact; decolonization, indigenization and equity; environmental sustainability; emerging as a vibrant hub; student centered approaches; innovation in learning teaching and research; and financial stewardship. The School of Graduate Studies invites applications for the following anticipated course offerings in the 2026-2027 academic year : SFIN-6021-301 Foresight Studio ( Fall 2026 Semester: September 9, 2026 – December 8, 2026; Wednesday, 6:30 p.m. - 9:30 p.m., Synchronous Online) COURSE DESCRIPTION: This studio course will introduce foresight methods in the development of strategic proposals in the private, public, voluntary or ‘for-benefit’ sector. Working in teams, students will identify an issue in a specific sector and will begin their exploration and research in a divergent process of signal discovery through methods such as environmental scanning, new technology research, user research, field study, or stakeholder workshops. This phase of work frames the problem. In a convergent process, students will apply methodologies, which include medium- to long-range scenario planning and technology adoption modelling to develop creative insights and implications for action. Students with credit in SFIN-6C02 may not take this course for credit. RESPONSIBILITIES: Teaching and teaching-related responsibilities may include but are not limited to: course outline development, studio preparation and delivery inclusive of lectures, critiques, grading, mid-term evaluations and final evaluations/reviews; including responsibility for health & safety and meeting academic and curricular requirements and deadlines. QUALIFICATIONS: The successful applicant should have a terminal degree in art, design or relevant discipline and/or equivalent professional/practical experience Experience teaching at the post-secondary level is considered an asset, equivalent relevant experience will be considered Outstanding interpersonal and communication skills Demonstrated leadership and experience promoting a respectful and inclusive work and learning environment for students, staff, and faculty TERMS OF EMPLOYMENT: Sessional appointments are fixed-term annual contracts. Sessional compensation includes a per-course pay scale with salary level of $7,508.00 ( per course) plus 7% in lieu of benefits and vacation pay, per the provisions of the Employment Standards Act. These positions are subject to budgetary approval and satisfactory enrolment levels. Applications should include: 1. A letter of intent stating the candidate’s interests in the areas of teaching by identifying relevant courses above; 2. A curriculum vitae; 3. A statement of teaching philosophy that responds to the profile of the position; 4. A link or web address to your digital portfolio of recent and current work/publications (if applicable) 5. And the names and contact information of three references. Please upload all five (5) components (including the URL to work) as one PDF . Incomplete applications will not be considered. Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code Interested applicants are invited to submit their application in confidence, addressed to School of Graduate Studies Appointments Committee, online, by clicking the “Apply Now” button below. The review of applications will begin on August 11, 2026 at 4:00pm and will continue until the position is filled. Applicants must be eligible to work in Canada for any employer (with a valid work or study permit, SIN and Canadian bank account). If you require application support, please contact the People & Culture Office. Please note that People & Culture cannot update you on the status of your application as these searches are decentralized to their respective Faculty offices. You will only be contacted if you are shortlisted for an interview. If the posting is advertised, we are still accepting applications. As an institution committed to art, design, digital media and related scholarship, OCAD University (OCAD U) recognizes the profound and essential value that diversity brings to the creation, reception and circulation of creative practices and discourse. OCAD U understands that valuing diverse creative practices and forms of knowledge are essential to and enrich the institution’s core mission and vision as an art and design university with a local and global scope. As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-deserving communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. We encourage members of designated equity-deserving groups to self-identify within the voluntary Applicant Questionnaire . In order to alleviate the under-representation of racialized and Indigenous faculty, priority in hiring will be given to qualified racialized and Indigenous persons who self-identify as such in the application process. This initiative is a special program under the Ontario Human Rights Code. OCAD U is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCAD U’s Policy of Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3862": {
      "title": "Position Description",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Position Description Tier II, Canada Research Chair: Artificial Intelligence and Creativity Job Code 3862 Department Research Office Employment Type Permanent Apply Now OCAD University (OCAD U) acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishnaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we gather and create. OCAD University, Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, is a vibrant community of bold, curious, and compassionate artists, designers and scholars who are imagining and creating a joyful, equitable, and sustainable world. The University is an internationally renowned hub for art, design, digital media, research, innovation and creativity, and arts administration. It embraces collaborative and interdisciplinary approaches to change-making through art, design-thinking curriculum, and research, making OCAD U a local, regional, national, and global leader in art and design. Students benefit from hands-on studio learning and have access to state-of-the-art shops and studios for both traditional and digital creation. Alongside a diverse and supportive team of instructors and peers, students gain employable skills. The School of Graduate Studies offers numerous programs where students may earn a Masters of Fine Arts or Masters of Design: Strategic Foresight and Innovation, Inclusive Design, Design for Health, Interdisciplinary Art/Media/Design, Digital Futures, and Criticism and Curatorial Practice. The University offers BA, BFA, and BDes degrees in Advertising, Creative Writing, Criticism and Curatorial Practice, Digital Futures, Drawing and Painting, Environmental Design, Experimental Animation, Graphic Design, Illustration, Indigenous Visual Culture, Industrial Design, Integrated Media, Interior Design, Material Art and Design, Photography, Printmaking and Publications, Scultpure/Installation, and Visual and Critical Studies. OCAD U graduates work in a variety of sectors such as advertising, art history and curatorial studies, digital futures, strategic foresight, inclusive design, health design, urban planning, environmental design, gaming, film, animation, publishing, illustration, graphic design, product design and consultancy, material-based design, visual arts, and arts administration. TENURE-TRACK POSITION: ASSISTANT/ASSOCIATE PROFESSOR TIER II CANADA RESEARCH CHAIR in Artificial Intelligence and Creativity Posted August 7, 2026 OCAD University invites applications for a full-time Tenure-Track Tier 2 Canada Research Chair in Artificial Intelligence and Creativity at the Assistant or Associate Professor rank. The position is expected to commence January 1 or July 1, 2028, and is subject to budgetary approval. In addition, this position is contingent on the candidate’s successful application to the Canada Research Chair (Tier 2) program. The successful candidate will be required to participate in preparation of a CRC nomination package for submission in April 2027. The successful candidate will demonstrate intersectional expertise in the context of or related to AI & Creativity in areas such as: generative art and computational creativity studio-based AI research, artist-led machine learning, creative coding and software art, digital fabrication and AI-assisted making, interactive and immersive media, performance and time-based AI practice, human-AI collaboration in creative process, critical making and speculative design, sound art and AI-generated composition, film, animation and AI cinematics, interactive and immersive media, game design and procedural world-building, wearable and embodied AI systems, decolonial approaches to creative AI, AI and craft-based practice, community-engaged creative AI practice, algorithmic justice, ethical making. Bringing together studio practice and computational research, the Canada Research Chair (CRC) in Artificial Intelligence and Creativity will provide visionary leadership for the development, critique, and application of artificial intelligence within expanded creative and artistic fields — centring the studio as a site of critical inquiry, cultural production, and technological imagination. The Canada Research Chair Tier 2 is for exceptional researchers, acknowledged by their peers as having the potential to lead in their field. The successful candidate should have experience as a Principal Investigator on at least one major, multi-year Tri-agency grant (NSERC, SSHRC or CIHR) or other major national/international academic grant, either held within the last five years or currently active. SUMMARY OF RESPONSIBILITIES The successful candidate will: Build research capacity and outputs in keeping with the OCAD University Strategic Research Plan and program criteria for a Tier 2 CRC ; Maintain an active research and practice profile with a focus on AI and Creativity; Demonstrate a broad understanding of the impact of AI on areas such as creative practice, business, health, climate change, social, and cultural contexts; Promote and augment interdisciplinary research as an individual and a leader of collaborations; Build and maintain internal and external networks of scholars, stakeholders and collaborators; Teach in one or more undergraduate and graduate programs within the most appropriate home faculty or across faculties; Assume a key role in curriculum development related to the AI and Creativity; Provide service to the Faculty and University-wide governance system, as well as activities and outreach initiatives that contribute to the quality of academic and student life and University culture; Develop a plan to attract major national and international grants to support their research program; Engage in development of policies and training at the University. QUALIFICATIONS The ideal candidate will have: A PhD in a field relevant to AI and Creativity; exceptional creative scholars/practitioners with a Masters degree will also be considered as a doctoral degree is not required by the Canada Research Chairs Program. A minimum of 5 years relevant teaching experience at the post-secondary level (or equivalent experience); Outstanding and innovative research skills and accomplishments which will lead them to a strong international reputation as a leader, innovator, and cultural practitioner with demonstrated commitment to AI and Creativity; An original and innovative cross-disciplinary research program of the highest quality. Experience and conceptual understanding of intersectional AI and Creativity practices; A significant research/exhibition and publication record consistent with a Tier II CRC including a proven successful track record with scholarly and/or arts granting councils Familiarity and proven funding success. Canadian applicants must show success with one or more of the Tri-agencies (SSHRC, NSERC or CIHR) demonstrated by holding principal investigator status on past or current Tri-agency grants; International candidates must show success with one or more major research grants at a federal or senior external level. Participating as co-applicant and/or collaborator on tri-agency or other national grants will be an asset. Experience supervising graduate students at the Masters level (PhD supervision experience is an asset) Capacity to attract excellent graduate students and future researchers as well as play a leadership role in developing new talent and increasing the research capacity of OCAD University; Experience in curriculum development and working to develop and enhance undergraduate and graduate programs A demonstrated commitment to the principles and lived experiences of equity and diversity, and proven ability to work effectively and collegially with a diverse population; A proven record of research and/or professional practice relevant to qualifications, experience and discipline; Experience proactively advancing equity and sustainability in curriculum, teaching, scholarship, and creative/professional activities; Demonstrated understanding of the ways in which equity, Indigenous knowledge, and sustainability are fundamental to the quality of student experience, to innovative scholarship, and to creative production; and A proven record of promoting a respectful and inclusive work and learning environment for students, staff, and faculty. Tier 2 Chairs are intended for exceptional emerging scholars. Applicants who are more than 10 years from their first independent academic research-related appointment may have their eligibility for a Tier 2 Chair assessed through the program’s Tier 2 justification process . Please contact research@ocadu.ca for more information. OCAD University recognizes that life circumstances such as illness, disability, family and community responsibilities (e.g., maternity leave, parental leave, leaves due to illness, leaves due to caring for family members, or slowdowns due to chronic illness or disability) may have an impact on a candidate’s record of research achievement.  These impacts will be taken into careful consideration during the assessment process. REMUNERATION Salary and rank will be commensurate with experience and qualifications with access to full benefits, and pension contribution after one year of service. Canada Research Chairs are subject to review and approval by the Tri-agency Institutional Programs Secretariat. APPLICATION REQUIREMENTS & DEADLINE Applications should include: A letter of intent stating the candidate’s interests in the areas of research, teaching, professional practice, and service to Faculty and University-wide governance; A curriculum vitae; A statement demonstrating the candidate’s research philosophy and equity/diversity work; Evidence of teaching and research effectiveness (such as sample assignments, student feedback, course evaluations, records of successful research grants, etc.); A link to a digital portfolio of recent work/publications; The names and contact information of three referees. Please upload all six (6) components (including the URL to work) as one PDF . Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code Interested applicants are invited to submit their electronic application through the portal in confidence addressed to: Ashok Mathur, Ph.D., Vice-President, Research & Dean, School of Graduate Studies OCAD University The review of applications will begin on September 30, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. As an institution committed to art, design, digital media and related scholarship, OCAD University recognizes the profound and essential value that diversity brings to the creation, reception and circulation of creative practices and discourse. OCAD University understands that valuing diverse creative practices and forms of knowledge are essential to, and enrich the institution’s core mission and vision as an art and design university with a local and global scope. As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity seeking communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. We encourage members of equity-seeking groups to self-identify within their letter of intent in their application . In order to alleviate the under-representation of racialized individuals and Indigenous persons in the ranks of tenure-track faculty and academic administrators, priority in hiring will be given to qualified persons who self-identify as such in the application process. This initiative is a special program under the Ontario Human Rights Code. OCAD University is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture or Alli O’Shea , People & Culture Advisor, for more information or refer to OCAD U’s Policy of Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3890": {
      "title": "Position Description",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Position Description Learning Skills Specialist Job Code 3890 Department Centre for Learning and Teaching Employment Type Temporary/Contract Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we live, work and create OCAD University , Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, is a vibrant community of bold, curious and compassionate artists, designers and scholars who are imagining and creating a joyful, equitable and sustainable world. The University is an internationally renowned hub for art, design, digital media, research, innovation and creativity, and arts administration. It embraces collaborative and interdisciplinary approaches to change-making through art, design-thinking curriculum and research, making OCAD U a local, regional, national and global leader in art and design. It is an exciting time to join the OCAD U community as the University is at a key stage in its institutional transformation, with the implementation of its Academic and Strategic Plan and guiding principles — driving positive impact; decolonization, indigenization and equity; environmental sustainability; emerging as a vibrant hub; student centered approaches; innovation in learning teaching and research; and financial stewardship. Reporting to the Manager, Student Writing, Communication and Learning Support, the Learning Skills Specialist develops, plans and delivers learning support programs for undergraduate and graduate students at OCAD University (OCAD U) to support their academic success and promote their engagement with scholarly, creative and disciplinary discourses and communities with an emphasis on reducing barriers to learning for disabled students, including those registered with Student Accessibility Services (SAS). The Learning Skills Specialist collaborates with program staff to build capacity for learning support across Centre programs and service areas and works closely with SAS staff to facilitate holistic student supports, contributing to initiatives that address the intersectional learning experiences and enhance access to learning for OCAD U’s diverse student body. This role broadly serves as a resource in the areas of student learning skills development and accessible student-centred pedagogy, informed by a commitment to decolonizing, anti-racist approaches and an understanding of Disability Studies theory and practice. The Learning Skills Specialist works within the Centre for Learning & Teaching (CLT) as part of a larger team delivering a range of programs and services for faculty, students, Teaching Assistants and other instructional staff. Together, they collaborate with the university community to provide leadership and support that creates and sustains equitable and engaging teaching and learning environments for all OCAD University students. This position is eligible for hybrid work arrangements; however, a minimum of three days per week of on campus work is required with flexibility to adjust the days and times of work on campus to meet programming needs. Summary of Responsibilities: Participate in portfolio-wide program planning processes with reference to emerging student needs, curricular changes and OCAD University’s Academic and Strategic Plan Develop and deliver learning support programs (e.g. one-on-one, small group, peer-based, co-curricular, classroom-based) to facilitate student learning skills development with an emphasis on supporting the success of disabled students; consult with SAS staff and other stakeholders to identify ongoing and emerging student needs and to inform program development and delivery priorities Provide individual learning skills consultations for students drawing from learning strategy best practice Develop and provide student support, resources and learning opportunities in the use of learning technologies and applications for navigating and reducing barriers to learning and enhancing academic success Develop and deliver learning skills training and resources for program staff as part of annual pre-service and in-service tutor education to build capacity for learning skills support across program and service areas In consultation with the Lead, Co-Curricular and Peer Learning Programs, develop and deliver student learning skills-building workshops as part of student transition, orientation and retention programming with an emphasis on supporting the success of disabled students; collaborate with SAS to develop and deliver academic orientation and transition programming for students with disabilities Collaborate with the Lead, Co-curricular and Peer Learning Programs to develop and deliver academic skills workshops for students on probation Work closely with SAS to deliver student programming and faculty supports, as required, that strengthens access and accommodations processes for disabled students, including students registered with SAS Support the delivery of undergraduate academic integrity consultations and other resources as required; complete related reporting requirements Develop and maintain student learning resources to support their success in in person, blended and online learning environments and across all course types (e.g. studio, seminar, lecture) In consultation with the Lead, Co-Curricular and Peer Learning Programs, contribute to the development and implementation of student learning support program policies and procedures Facilitate timely and effective student referrals within the Centre and between the Centre, SAS and other areas of the university Develop web content as required and contribute to promotional and outreach initiatives related to learning skills programs and services Undertake research to identify promising practices and provide expertise and guidance across the staff team and the University to support the integration of evidence-based practice; contribute to faculty and staff professional learning opportunities to promote best practices in student learning skills development and accessible  pedagogy, informed by equity-based and Disability Studies approaches to education, as required Contribute to the development and implementation of program monitoring and evaluation tools and participate in critically reflective practice and evidence-informed program development and decision-making Actively contribute to Learning Skills professional communities and conversations as well as best practice investigations and advancements in learning skills development and accessible pedagogy for art and design education; share research findings through a variety of knowledge dissemination activities Participate in reflective practice and professional learning activities, individually and in teams, to integrate equity and sustainability principles across relevant programs, processes and practices Fulfill Centre and Ministry reporting requirements Participate in departmental planning in support of the university’s strategic and academic plans and Centre goals and objectives; participate on relevant permanent or ad-hoc University committees, as required Assist the Manager and Director in other related tasks that contribute to the successful operation of the Centre and support the Centre’s strategic directions Qualifications: Master’s degree in Education, Social Sciences or equivalent; Supplemental Instruction, Learning Disability Specialist or Learning Strategist training certificate an asset A minimum of five (5) years of experience providing learning and academic skills building programs to diverse learners and students with disabilities in a post-secondary environment; progressive experience working in a university academic success centre or equivalent In-depth understanding of the learning and academic support needs of diverse learners and students with disabilities Post-secondary studies in Art or Design an asset; past or present art or design practice an asset Strong knowledge of best practices in student learning theory and practice; demonstrated experience developing and implementing co-curricular peer learning programming Informed understanding of the opportunities, limitations and risks associated with artificial intelligence (AI) for student learning and academic success; active engagement with evolving research, pedagogical practices and sector developments related to AI use in learning skills education and support. Demonstrated understanding of critical pedagogy, equity studies, accessible pedagogy and Universal Design for Learning (UDL); understanding of Disability Studies theory and practices with experience translating Disability Studies approaches into student program development and program delivery practices Excellent program development skills with experience conducting needs assessments and stakeholder engagement to inform multi-year program planning Demonstrated experience designing, delivering and evaluating skills-building workshops for students; excellent group facilitation and instructional skills Demonstrated capacity for and experience in providing assistance to students in app and learning technology use for learning and academic success; experience giving students support in the use of assistive technology, an asset Excellent communication, interpersonal and leadership skills, and a demonstrated ability to exercise tact, sound judgment and commitment to confidentiality Excellent academic and professional writing skills with the ability to write effectively for different audiences Demonstrated experience producing educational resources and handouts including experience developing learning resources to support online learning environments Knowledge of best practices in multilingual pedagogy and an understanding of the barriers and support needs of post-secondary students studying in a second language Track record of working consultatively with faculty to build relationships and support student program development and delivery; experience developing and delivering faculty and TA professional learning programming an asset Demonstrated experience developing and integrating decolonizing, anti-racist, accessible and translingual approaches and pedagogies in previous work or study Demonstrated commitment to the principles of equity, anti-racism, decolonization and accessibility; and experience promoting a respectful work and learning environment for students, staff and faculty Demonstrated ability to take initiative, excellent organizational and time management skills Strong computer skills with knowledge of Microsoft 365 applications; knowledge of Canvas learning management system and Adobe Creative Cloud applications an asset Flexibility to adjust hours and location of work as needed to support programs and events outside of regular office hours and location This posting is for an existing vacancy. Mode of Work: Hybrid. A minimum of three days per week of on campus work is required with flexibility to adjust the days and times of work on campus to meet programming needs. Compensation: Hiring Range - $42.81 to $49.08 per hour commensurate with experience, plus 4% vacation pay and 7% in lieu of *benefits; Salary Range - $42.81 to $56.26 per hour. * The successful incumbent has the option of pay in lieu or enrollment in benefits. Hours of Work: Monday to Friday, for a total of 35 hours per week. This is a temporary contract expected to continue until June 5 th , 2028. Application Deadline: Interested applicants are invited to submit an updated * resume and cover letter ( PDF ) by selecting \"Apply Now\" below. Review of applications will begin on October 1, 2026 and continue until the position is filled * Please upload your resume and cover letter as one PDF . Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code As an institution committed to art, design, digital media and related scholarship, OCAD University recognizes the profound and essential value that diversity brings to the creation, reception and circulation of creative practices and discourse. OCAD University understands that valuing diverse creative practices and forms of knowledge are essential to and enrich the institution’s core mission and vision as an art and design university with a local and global scope. As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-deserving communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. OCAD University is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCAD U’s Policy of Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities . All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3884": {
      "title": "Position Description",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Position Description Privacy Specialist Job Code 3884 Department Office of the Vice-President, Finance & Administration Employment Type Permanent Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we live, work and create OCAD University , Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, is a vibrant community of bold, curious and compassionate artists, designers and scholars who are imagining and creating a joyful, equitable and sustainable world. The University is an internationally renowned hub for art, design, digital media, research, innovation and creativity, and arts administration. It embraces collaborative and interdisciplinary approaches to change-making through art, design-thinking curriculum and research, making OCAD U a local, regional, national and global leader in art and design. It is an exciting time to join the OCAD U community as the University is at a key stage in its institutional transformation, with the implementation of its Academic and Strategic Plan and guiding principles — driving positive impact; decolonization, indigenization and equity; environmental sustainability; emerging as a vibrant hub; student centered approaches; innovation in learning teaching and research; and financial stewardship. Reporting to the Vice-President, Finance and Administration (VPFA), the Privacy Specialist acts as OCAD University’s subject matter expert on matters related to access to information, protection of privacy, and records management. Responsibilities include proper application of access and privacy legislation, processing access to information requests, promoting proper records retention and good record-keeping practices, completing privacy and security impact assessments, investigating privacy breaches and privacy complaints, and promoting a culture of privacy protection and good information governance at the University. The Privacy Specialist operates independently and exercises professional judgment while keeping the VPFA informed of significant privacy and records management matters, breaches, investigations, compliance issues and emerging institutional risks, and is responsible for ensuring the University is in compliance with relevant privacy and access legislation. The Specialist must have knowledge of legislative requirements and must maintain an ongoing awareness of developments in the areas of privacy and records management, and the implications of those developments for the University. Summary of Responsibilities : Privacy (70%) Independently provide authoritative advice on the University’s obligations under access and privacy legislation Ontario’s Freedom of Information and Protection of Privacy Act (FIPPA) and Personal Health Information Protection Act (PHIPA) are collectively referred to as “Privacy Law”. Prepare regular reports, analyses and strategic advice for the VPFA regarding privacy compliance, records management, emerging risks, breaches, trends and information governance matters Develop institutional privacy frameworks and provide strategic advice and recommendations regarding privacy, information governance and related institutional risks Provide information, guidance and advice to the University community and general public regarding questions about privacy and information access requests at the University Respond to the University’s access for information requests, ensuring compliance with Privacy Law Provide guidance and information to requestors (both internal and external to the University) with their access requests Direct and coordinate the retrieval of information relevant to access requests with staff, faculty and administration Prepare responses to requests for information through review and consolidation of retrieved information Interpret Privacy Law in order to make informed decisions regarding the application of exemptions and exclusions to the information requested, if applicable, including researching relevant orders and judicial review decisions Respond and act on behalf of the University when decisions on access are appealed to the Information and Privacy Commissioner of Ontario (IPC) Receive and investigate privacy-related complaints, and respond to the IPC assigned analyst in cases of formal privacy complaints against the University Lead the response to privacy breaches at the University through the collection of relevant information and providing subject matter expertise to senior administrators for informed decision-making Work with IT in cases of cybersecurity incidents involving personal information. Coordinate any required involvement from legal counsel through liaising with the Office of the Vice-President, Finance and Administration Submit all mandatory reporting to the IPC and prepares annual reports on Privacy Law compliance for the Audit, Finance and Risk Committee of the Board of Governors Plan, lead and conduct Privacy Impact Assessments Work closely with Information Technology staff to evaluate and provide authoritative recommendations on Privacy and Security Impact Assessments for IT solutions Provide advice and recommendations on information technology projects, privacy implications of emerging technologies (i.e. AI solutions/applications) or other initiatives involving personal information being implemented across or in different units at the University, which may have an impact on privacy, confidentiality, or records retention Collaborate and communicate with privacy and access colleagues at other universities across Ontario to ensure best practices and up-to-date methods are maintained Keep up-to-date with new developments in Privacy Law, as well as directives from the IPC office Act as privacy/information subject matter expert on relevant committees and working groups Develop and provide training to member of the University community about their obligations under Privacy Law Records Management (30%) Develop and maintain the University’s records management framework by actively participating on the Records and Information Management Advisory Committee and overseeing existing policies and procedures as they relate to the RIM program Provides leadership and guidance to the University community on records management and retention best practices Advise on and recommend best practices for records management to departments, particularly in relation with Privacy Law Provide advice and assistance to units in the implementation of records management procedures and practices, including assisting with file plans, retention schedules, and the management of records Advise senior administration on records management issues as they affect long-term reliability and accessibility of University records Act as subject matter expert on record management on relevant committees and working groups Promote and encourage awareness of the role and importance of records management in the University community through training, education and effective communication Monitor and audit the records management program at the University Stay up-to-date on best practices in the field of records management and information governance Continually monitor changes and new developments in provincial and federal statutes and regulations that affect the management and retention of University records Qualifications : Undergraduate degree required; a graduate degree and/or certification in a related field (e.g. CIPP/C, CRM, MLIS) is considered an asset Minimum of six (6) years of experience in the field of privacy and records management, preferably in a public sector environment Experience reviewing and interpreting relevant statutes, regulations and case law Extensive knowledge of FIPPA and PHIPA, with some knowledge of Canada’s Anti-Spam Legislation, General Data Protection Regulation, Personal Information Protection and Electronic Documents Act and related legislation and regulations Demonstrated research and analytical skills Demonstrated ability to deal with sensitive and confidential information in an appropriate and effective manner Ability to determine the dissemination of various types of sensitive and/or confidential information Strong communication skills, including the ability to explain complex matters in plain language and the ability to write clearly and succinctly in a variety of formats (policies, procedures, bulletins, training guides, etc.) Expected to be self-directed in responding to requests, complaints, breaches, and questions Exceptional organizational skills and the ability to work independently while coordinating one’s own work with that of several offices Professional demeanor and ability to relate effectively with senior administrators, staff, faculty and external stakeholders Thorough knowledge of records management theory and methodology Ability to prioritize activities, recognize urgent matters amongst multiple requests, all with competing deadlines and prioritizes information that needs to be dealt with immediately Familiarity with information technology as it relates to matters of privacy, security and protection of personal information Strong project management and change management skills Strong focus on excellence in customer service and project leadership Understanding of the business requirements of an academic institution and the ability to identify the needs of university departments Demonstrated commitment to the principles of equity and diversity and experience promoting an inclusive work and learning environment Must be willing and able to provide regular flexibility with respect to hours of work Mode of Work: Hybrid Compensation: Hiring Range - $84,311.75 to $96,656.35 per annum, commensurate with experience, plus benefits; Salary Range - $84,311.75 to $110,808.36 per annum. Hours of Work : Monday to Friday, 35 hours per week. Application Deadline: Interested applicants are invited to submit an updated * resume and cover letter ( PDF )by selecting \"Apply Now\" below, no later than Monday September 21 st , 2026. * Please upload your resume and cover letter as one PDF . Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-deserving communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. OCAD University is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCAD U’s Policy of Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
