    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3895": {
      "title": "Position Description",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Position Description Employee Wellness Specialist Job Code 3895 Department People & Culture Employment Type Temporary/Contract Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we live, work and create OCAD University , Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, is a vibrant community of bold, curious and compassionate artists, designers and scholars who are imagining and creating a joyful, equitable and sustainable world. The University is an internationally renowned hub for art, design, digital media, research, innovation and creativity, and arts administration. It embraces collaborative and interdisciplinary approaches to change-making through art, design-thinking curriculum and research, making OCAD U a local, regional, national and global leader in art and design. It is an exciting time to join the OCAD U community as the University is at a key stage in its institutional transformation, with the implementation of its Academic and Strategic Plan and guiding principles — driving positive impact; decolonization, indigenization and equity; environmental sustainability; emerging as a vibrant hub; student centered approaches; innovation in learning teaching and research; and financial stewardship. Reporting to the Manager, People Strategies, the Employee Wellness Specialist is responsible for organization-wide programs focused on employee wellbeing, leaves of absence, human rights-based accommodation and return-to-work in keeping with OCAD University’s (OCAD U) strategic and academic plans and values. The Specialist provides advice and guidance to managers, employees, and others within the People & Culture office, and develops organizational policies and strategies in consultation with university stakeholders. This role also supports the implementation of formal processes and procedures to support the success of all OCAD U employees, with specific regard for the success of equity-deserving groups. Summary of Responsibilities: Leave, Accommodation & Disability Case Management In concert with managers and academic leaders, administer medical leaves of absence for all employee groups, including collecting required medical documentation, monitoring leave timelines, preparing documentation for benefits provider(s) and coordinating return to work arrangements, as applicable Provide end-to-end case management for leave, disability and accommodation matters, from intake and assessment through implementation, monitoring, return to work and case closure, ensuring timely communication, appropriate documentation and consistent application of university requirements. Manage complex disability management cases from onset to return to work; identifying opportunities for early intervention and safe, sustainable return to work strategies.  Administer the full range of statutory, contractual, medical and University-approved leaves of absence for all employee groups, applying relevant legislation, collective agreements, employment terms and University policies consistently. Maintain awareness of client group needs and support the P&C Advisor with workforce planning while supporting leave management. Coordinate and provide guidance on employee and applicant accommodation requests relating to disability and other protected grounds under the Ontario Human Rights Code, in accordance with applicable legislation and University policy. Facilitate the development of individualized accommodation plans based on an understanding of functional limitations and operational needs; implement, evaluate and modify accommodations as appropriate to support with successful re-boarding of employees. Research and assess accommodation and return-to-work options; provide evidence-informed recommendations to managers and academic leaders; document case decisions and rationales; and escalate complex, disputed or high-risk matters as appropriate. Negotiate and act on sensitive and complex issues pertaining to employee accommodations, initiating consultations and case conferences where necessary Maintain complete, accurate and secure leave and accommodation records, protecting medical and personal information and limiting disclosure to information reasonably required to administer the leave or implement the accommodation. In accordance with the university’s Accommodation in Employment Policy, coordinate applicant and employee accommodation needs; liaise with the Human Rights & Equity Office and Safety, Security & Campus Operations where appropriate Employee Wellbeing Research best practices, trends and new initiatives in employee wellness to inform and improve current programs Support in-house training initiatives related to wellness, human rights and equity, including assisting with development of annual training plan and facilitating information sessions, as required Participate as management-designated representative on the Joint Health & Safety Committee, as needed Develop and implement related policies, tools and training Evaluate, measure, and report on the effectiveness of wellness programs; make recommendations for changes Provide high-level customer service by ensuring seamless coordination of work with other People & Culture staff Manage related budget(s) and make recommendations for effective allocation of funds Assist the Manager, People Strategies in related functions and projects contributing to the successful operation of the People & Culture office Research & Policy Support Prepare internal and external reports as required; liaise with other departments and P&C staff to coordinate and collect related employee leave related data Maintain and analyze leave and accommodation case data to monitor service delivery, identify systemic trends and barriers, support workforce planning, and recommend improvements to policies, processes and preventative strategies. Conduct various cyclical audits to gain insights and establish needs as it relates to leave and accommodation management across the university Maintain an accurate electronic filing system as it relates to leaves and accommodation, and perform related tasks to ensure records are easily accessible to appropriate staff and kept in accordance with guidelines Conduct employee research and analysis in leave and accommodation best practices across the post-secondary sector. Using the content management system, review and update P&C section of the OCAD U website and respective SharePoint site, ensuring compliance with AODA standards Serve as a backup for other administrative and coordination functions within the People & Culture team, as needed Qualifications: A post-secondary degree in Human Resources, Business or a related field, or an equivalent combination of education and experience with special consideration for the professional and community-based experience of equity-deserving groups A Certified Human Resources Leader (CHRL) designation is preferred Minimum of four (4) years of work experience in accommodations, disability management, and return to work programs or related functions, preferable within a unionized environment and/or a post-secondary or public sector organization Sound Knowledge of HR policies and practices, collective agreements, and relevant employment legislation, such as the Employment Standards Act (ESA), Ontario Labour Relations Act (OLRA), Ontario Human Rights Code (OHRC), Occupational Health and Safety Act, and the Accessibility for Ontarians with Disabilities Act (AODA) Knowledge of both HR trends, and academic issues is preferred. Demonstrated approach that centers around employee wellbeing and legal responsibilities with a demonstrated commitment to the principles of equity, diversity, and inclusion A collaborative and consultative working style with an ability to develop and maintain effective working relationships in a manner that gains trust, respect and cooperation Excellent communication and interpersonal skills and judgement, with a demonstrated ability to deal with sensitive or confidential matters with discretion Qualities that include integrity, openness, fairness, resourcefulness Strong computer skills and a working knowledge of all Microsoft Office applications and well-developed internet research skills; knowledge of Human Resources Information Systems (HRIS) Demonstrated ability to work independently and participate collaboratively in a team environment This posting is for an existing vacancy. Mode of Work: Hybrid Compensation: Hiring Range - $42.81 to $49.08 per hour commensurate with experience, plus 4% vacation pay and 7% in lieu of benefits; Salary Range - $42.81 to $56.26 per hour. Hours of Work: Monday to Friday, 35 hours per week. This is a temporary contract with an expected duration of 6 months. Application Deadline: Interested applicants are invited to submit an updated * resume and cover letter ( PDF ) by selecting \"Apply Now\" below. The review of applications will begin Wednesday October 14 th and will continue until the position has been filled. * Please upload your resume and cover letter as one PDF . Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code As an institution committed to art, design, digital media and related scholarship, OCAD University recognizes the profound and essential value that diversity brings to the creation, reception and circulation of creative practices and discourse. OCAD University understands that valuing diverse creative practices and forms of knowledge are essential to and enrich the institution’s core mission and vision as an art and design university with a local and global scope. As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-deserving communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. OCAD University is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCADU’s Accommodation in Employment Policy . All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3896": {
      "title": "Position Description",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Position Description Executive Assistant, Office of the Vice-President, Academic & Provost (VPAP) Job Code 3896 Department Office of the Vice-President, Academic & Provost Employment Type Permanent Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we live, work and create OCAD University is seeking a seasoned and highly capable Executive Assistant to join the Office of the Vice-President, Academic & Provost (VPAP). This is a high-level executive support role for someone who can step into a busy office, quickly understand what matters, and take ownership. We are looking for an experienced EA who is proactive, confident, exceptionally organized, and comfortable working alongside a senior executive in a fast-paced and dynamic environment. The successful candidate will anticipate needs and take initiative to keep priorities moving forward. You know how to manage competing demands, exercise sound judgement, identify what requires attention, and ensure important work stays on track. You are comfortable following up, asking the right questions, and respectfully ensuring that information, decisions and action items move forward. Reporting to the Director, Academic Affairs & Senate Secretary, and functionally to the Vice-President, Academic & Provost, you will be a key partner in the effective operation of the VPAP Office. What You’ll Do At the centre of the role is the effective management of the VPAP’s time, information, priorities and commitments. Responsibilities include: Own and actively manage the VPAP’s complex calendar, anticipating scheduling needs, resolving conflicts, coordinating high-level internal and external meetings, and ensuring the VPAP is prepared for upcoming commitments. Manage and triage the VPAP’s email inbox, identifying urgent and high-priority matters, tracking deadlines and follow-ups, responding to inquiries as directed, and ensuring important items do not fall through the cracks. Anticipate what the VPAP needs to be successful, compiling briefing materials, agendas, minutes, reports, updates and relevant background information in advance of meetings and decisions. Identify and escalate priority issues and, working with the VPAP and Director, coordinate appropriate follow-up to keep matters moving toward resolution. Draft, edit and coordinate correspondence, communications, presentations, reports and other materials on behalf of the VPAP and the Office. Handle highly confidential and sensitive information with exceptional judgement and discretion, including materials related to budgets, grievances and labour relations. Build strong working relationships across the academic portfolio and throughout the University, ensuring effective communication and coordination between the VPAP Office and its many partners. Liaise confidently and professionally with senior administrators, faculty, staff, students, external stakeholders, government officials and members of the public on behalf of the VPAP. Serve as an initial point of contact for complex or sensitive matters brought to the VPAP Office, using sound judgement to determine the appropriate pathway for resolution. Provide administrative and coordination support to academic committees, ad hoc committees and working groups, including committees identified under the University's Memorandum of Agreement. Conduct research and support analysis related to academic initiatives, institutional priorities and special projects. Prepare, monitor and reconcile VPAP Office budgets and related financial activities, including invoices and purchasing card statements. Lead the coordination of academic events, including conferences, receptions, workshops and other special initiatives. Develop and maintain effective electronic and administrative systems that support an organized, responsive executive office. Coordinate travel and accommodation arrangements as required. Serve as backup to the Quality Assurance Officer & Associate Senate Secretary, as required. Take on other responsibilities that contribute to the effective operation of the VPAP Office. Who We’re Looking For You are an experienced Executive Assistant who is comfortable operating with a high degree of independence and accountability. You assess what is happening, determine what needs attention, and take initiative to move the work forward. You are likely to thrive in this role if you: Have significant experience supporting a senior executive in a complex, fast-paced environment. Are proactive and solutions-oriented, with a natural instinct to anticipate needs and stay ahead of priorities. Are confident taking ownership of an executive’s calendar, inbox, priorities and follow-ups. Are a committed team player who wants to be an active and equally contributing member of a small, highly collaborative team. You pitch in when priorities shift and care about the success of the Office as a whole. Are able to successfully manage a high-volume workload in an environment characterized by frequent interruptions, evolving priorities and tight deadlines. Can comfortably and diplomatically follow up with senior leaders, obtain the information you need, and keep commitments and timelines moving forward. Know when to act independently, when to consult, and when something needs to be escalated. Are exceptionally organized and able to create order when priorities are shifting quickly. Bring confidence, professionalism, sound judgement and discretion to your work, and take pride in being someone who keeps things moving, closes loops and gets things done. What You Bring A post-secondary degree or an equivalent combination of education and experience. At least four (4) years of Executive Assistant experience, including direct support to senior executives. Experience in post-secondary education is an asset. Advanced proficiency with Microsoft Office applications, particularly Outlook and Teams, and the ability to quickly learn new systems and technology. Strong writing, research and financial administration skills, including experience preparing executive-level communications and working with budgets. A demonstrated commitment to equity and diversity and to fostering a respectful work and learning environment. The flexibility to occasionally adjust your hours to support the needs of a busy executive office. This posting is for an existing vacancy. Mode of Work: On Campus This position is expected to work on campus four (4) days per week, with one (1) remote workday. On-campus requirements may increase based on the operational needs of the unit. Compensation: Hiring Range - $66,925.74 to $76,724.74 per annum, commensurate with experience, plus benefits; Salary Range - $66,925.74 to $87,958.44 per annum. Hours of Work : Monday to Friday, 35 hours per week. Application Deadline: Interested applicants are invited to submit an updated * resume and cover letter ( PDF )by selecting \"Apply Now\" below. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position has been filled. * Please upload your resume and cover letter as one PDF . Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-deserving communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. OCAD University is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCADU’s Accommodation in Employment Policy All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3906": {
      "title": "Position Description",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Position Description Admissions Advisor Job Code 3906 Department Admissions & Recruitment Employment Type Permanent Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we live, work and create Please note that internal job postings are open only to current permanent OPSEU Local 576 Unit 1 employees and temporary OPSEU Local 576 Unit 1 employees with one year of continuous service. Reporting to the Manager, Undergraduate Admissions and working in collaboration with the Admissions & Recruitment team, the Admission Advisor provides accurate information and supports applicants for admission to undergraduate programs at OCAD University (OCAD U). The Admission Advisor is primarily responsible for all aspects and processes for undergraduate applications, including processing, evaluating, and maintaining academic records, responding to requests for assistance in completing the process, advising applicants on meeting requirements, advising applicants on the transition to postsecondary studies, as well as providing resources for housing, relocating to Toronto and scholarship opportunities. The Admission Advisor reviews portfolio submissions and makes admission decisions.  This position also assists the Graduate Studies admissions team, working in various student information systems, aiding in the setup of the application cycle, handling documents, and assisting with GPA calculations. The incumbent builds and maintains strong relationships with providers in the areas of English language programs, academic upgrading, housing, scholarships and awards and other resources as identified to support the transition of applicants to student status at OCAD University (OCAD U). Summary of Responsibilities : Work with the Manager and departmental staff, OCAD U students, faculty, and staff to ensure current knowledge of all programs, admission policies, procedures, and entrance requirements Support prospective students and applicants throughout the admission process, advise applicants on academic, admission and portfolio/supplemental requirements, and provide guidance in the transition to student status Update and maintain applicant records using the University’s Student Information System (SIS) and audit data for accuracy in accordance with applicant documentation from the Ontario Universities’ Application Centre (OUAC), maintain accurate automated and manual filing systems Assess applicant files for academic entrance requirements including averages, GPAs, course requirements, and language proficiency for a diverse range of applicants from multiple education systems and countries Conduct reviews of applicant records throughout the admission cycle and before files are finalized and student records are created Assess portfolios and supplemental applicant materials and make admission decisions for undergraduate applicants along with the review team. Escalate issues (plagiarism, program suitability, etc.) as needed. Refer relevant applicants to the transfer admissions team for transfer credit consideration and assist in transfer credit assessment, processing and advising as needed.During peak times in the admissions process, assist the Graduate Admissions team with GPA calculations, systems setup and testing, document processing and verification, and advising graduate applicants as needed Work with the Admissions & Recruitment team on the development and maintenance of web content, publications (online and in print), training manuals and best practices for supporting applicants Respond to telephone, e-mail, virtual and in-person inquiries regarding the admissions process, requirements and admission decisions from prospective students, parents, guidance counsellors, teachers, and the general public Generate, disseminate, and maintain historical records of applicant files and correspondence Activate relationships with external housing partners and provide referrals to applicants in search of housing options Work with the English for Art & Design (EAD) Program Coordinator to ensure that eligible applicants can register for the English for Art & Design (EAD) program Research and maintain knowledge of English Language Pathway partner programs, including start/end dates and entry requirements, and refer applicants as needed Research and maintain knowledge of external entrance scholarship and award opportunities, and make referrals to applicants in search of funding options Refer applicants to transition programming available to incoming students through other OCAD U departments (i.e. Campus Life, Writing & Learning Centre, Registrar’s Office, Student Accessibility Services, etc.) Support the Admissions team and Office of the Registrar with new student registration assistance, as neededWork with Admissions & Recruitment team members, the OUAC and OCAD U IT/ERP staff to identify and resolve application and technical issues Provide high quality service and respond to telephone, email, virtual and in- person inquiries, and ensure that admissions activities are consistent with the established structures and policies, as well as standards of quality and customer service Participate in overall strategic admissions and recruitment planning, developing, and implementing student admissions initiatives and special projects, portfolio review events, online and in-person information sessions, and communications Represent OCAD U at on-campus and off-campus student recruitment events, including portfolio review events, as required Participate in the hiring, training, scheduling and supervision of student monitors, volunteers, and casual staff supporting the department, ensuring relevant Health & Safety training has been provided Contribute to the development of diversity, equity, inclusivity, and accessibility (DEIA) strategies throughout all admissions programs, services, and communications Support the Admissions & Recruitment team in all duties contributing to the successful operation of the department Qualifications: Postsecondary degree in art & design, or equivalent Minimum of four (4) years of administrative experience, preferably in a postsecondary educational environment, or equivalent Familiarity with Ontario, Canadian and International secondary and postsecondary school systems, proven knowledge of admission requirements, admission protocols, scholarship opportunities, external housing facilities and language and literacy concepts essential Demonstrated commitment to providing outstanding customer service for a diverse population of prospective students Demonstrated computer skills, with working knowledge of Microsoft Office applications with attention to spreadsheet, database, and reporting applications. Experience working with Student Information Systems (Ellucian Colleague) Experience working with Customer Relationship Management systems (Slate) Demonstrated communication and interpersonal skills, with the ability to work collaboratively in a team environment Demonstrated attention to detail and organizational skills, including the ability to prioritize tasks and meet deadlines Possession of a valid Ontario driver’s licence and availability to travel and/or work evenings and/or weekends as required Demonstrated commitment to the principles of equity and diversity and experience promoting a respectful work and learning environment for students, staff, and faculty Strong mathematical and accounting abilities; proficient writing and editing skills Excellent interpersonal, communication, organizational and analytical skills Demonstrated ability to take initiative and work independently and participate collaboratively in a team environment Compensation: OPSEU Unit I, Appendix A, Grade A4; $57,486.45 to $75,566.13 per annum Hours of Work: Monday to Friday, for a total of 35 hours per week. Mode of Work: This position is eligible for hybrid work arrangements; however, a minimum of three days per week of on-campus work is required, with flexibility to adjust on-campus days and hours to meet department needs. Occasional evening and weekend work will be required, with shift premiums applied in accordance with the Collective Agreement. All flexibility and scheduling provisions are subject to the terms outlined in the Collective Agreement. This posting is for an existing vacancy. Interested internal permanent OPSEU Local 576 Unit 1 or temporary OPSEU Local 576 Unit 1 employees with one year of continuous service applicants are invited to submit an updated *resume and cover letter ( PDF ) by selecting “Apply Now” below, no later than Thursday October 22, 2026. * Please upload your resume and cover letter as one PDF . Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-deserving communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. OCAD University is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCADU’s Accommodation in Employment Policy . All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3899": {
      "title": "Position Description",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Position Description Director, Strategic Communications Job Code 3899 Department Marketing & Communications Employment Type Temporary/Contract Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we live, work and create OCAD University , Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, is a vibrant community of bold, curious and compassionate artists, designers and scholars who are imagining and creating a joyful, equitable and sustainable world. The University is an internationally renowned hub for art, design, digital media, research, innovation and creativity, and arts administration. It embraces collaborative and interdisciplinary approaches to change-making through art, design-thinking curriculum and research, making OCAD U a local, regional, national and global leader in art and design. It is an exciting time to join the OCAD U community as the University is at a key stage in its institutional transformation, with the implementation of its Academic and Strategic Plan and guiding principles — driving positive impact; decolonization, indigenization and equity; environmental sustainability; emerging as a vibrant hub; student centered approaches; innovation in learning teaching and research; and financial stewardship. The Director, Strategic Communications leads OCAD University's (OCAD U) strategic communications function, developing and delivering integrated communications strategies that strengthen the University's brand, build public understanding and advance institutional priorities. Reporting to the Executive Director, Marketing & Communications, and working closely with the Director, Institutional Communications, the Director leads the University's proactive and strategic executive communications, internal communications, public relations, issues management, storytelling and content strategy. The successful candidate is first and foremost an exceptional communications leader. They recognize that communications practice has fundamentally evolved, and understand that communications, marketing and audience strategy are no longer separate disciplines, but complementary tools used to build awareness, trust, engagement and institutional value. They bring an audience-first mindset, balancing strategic insight with creativity, sound judgment and operational excellence. The successful candidate understands that the role of communications is no longer simply to inform. It is to influence understanding, earn attention and build long-term institutional value. Summary of Responsibilities: Strategic Communications Leadership Work with the Director, Institutional Communications to lead and develop a high-performing communications team, fostering creativity, collaboration, accountability and continuous improvement. Develop and execute integrated communications strategies that enhance OCAD University's reputation, strengthen its brand and advance institutional priorities. Translate institutional priorities into communications strategies that create measurable impact across multiple audiences. Establish priorities, allocate resources and ensure the successful delivery of multiple concurrent initiatives. Measure communications effectiveness and continuously refine strategies using research, audience insights and analytics. Brand, Narrative & Audience Strategy Lead the development and stewardship of OCAD University's narrative, ensuring communications consistently reinforce the University's distinctive brand. Translate complex academic, financial and institutional priorities into clear, compelling and audience-centred stories. Develop communications strategies informed by audience insight, research and analytics, ensuring messages resonate with different target audiences. Ensure every significant communication contributes to strengthening how OCAD University is understood, experienced and valued. Integrated Communications Lead the planning and execution of media relations, executive communications, internal communications, issues management and digital content. Develop integrated communications plans that complement institutional marketing strategies and reinforce a consistent institutional voice across earned, owned, shared and paid channels. Identify opportunities to amplify stories, campaigns and institutional priorities through coordinated communications and marketing efforts. Ensure communications are timely, relevant, audience-focused and aligned with institutional objectives. Executive Communications & Strategic Counsel Serve as a trusted communications advisor to the President, Executive Director and senior leadership. Develop speeches, presentations, briefing materials, key messages and executive communications, particularly focusing on change management. Provide strategic counsel on the communications implications of institutional decisions and emerging issues. Support senior leaders in building credibility and influence across higher education, the creative sector and public life. Reputation & Issues Management Anticipate reputational opportunities and risks and develop proactive communications strategies. Lead communications planning during complex, high-profile and sensitive issues. Develop messaging frameworks, stakeholder communications and executive briefing materials during periods of organizational change or crisis. Protect and strengthen the University's reputation through thoughtful, timely and audience-centred communications. Storytelling & Content Leadership Champion exceptional storytelling that reflects OCAD University's creativity, impact and distinctive character. Foster a culture of curiosity, experimentation and continuous improvement within the communications team. Identify stories that deepen audience engagement and reinforce institutional priorities. Ensure content is compelling, accessible and optimized for the platforms and audiences it serves. Qualifications: Undergraduate degree in Communications, Journalism, Public Relations, Marketing or a related discipline. Minimum of five (5) years of progressively senior communications leadership experience. Demonstrated success leading strategic communications within a complex organization – agencies, cultural institutions, government, purpose-driven organizations, media organizations and the private sector who bring a contemporary perspective on communications and audience engagement Exceptional experience in executive communications, media relations, issues management and organizational storytelling. Experience leading and developing high-performing teams. Demonstrated ability to advise senior executives and influence organizational decision-making. Experience developing integrated communications strategies informed by audience insight, brand thinking and marketing principles. Demonstrated experience building awareness and engagement through earned, owned, shared and paid communications. Knowledge, Skills & Competencies: The successful candidate will demonstrate: Exceptional strategic thinking and judgment. Outstanding writing, editing and storytelling abilities. Deep understanding of contemporary communications practice, including the integration of communications, marketing, audience strategy, brand and digital engagement to achieve organizational objectives. Demonstrated ability to translate complex ideas into clear, compelling and audience-centred communications. Strong understanding of contemporary media, digital platforms, audience behaviour and content strategy. Political acuity and the ability to navigate complex stakeholder environments with confidence and diplomacy. Strong leadership, coaching and relationship-building skills. Excellent project management and organizational abilities. Exercises excellent judgment in the use of AI as an integral part of professional communications practice, leveraging it to improve research, synthesis, planning, content development and decision-making while maintaining the highest standards of accuracy, originality and editorial quality. Demonstrated commitment to the principles of equity and diversity, and proven ability to deal effectively with a diverse population Mode of Work: Hybrid Compensation: $113,519.00 to $133,527.00 per year - commensurate with experience, plus 4% vacation pay and 7% in lieu of *benefits * The successful incumbent has the option of pay in lieu or enrollment in benefits. Hours of Work: Monday to Friday, 35 hours per week. This is a temporary contract with an expected duration of 1 year with possibility of renewal Application Deadline: Interested applicants are invited to submit an updated * resume and cover letter ( PDF ) by selecting \"Apply Now\" below. The review of applications will begin Thursday October 22 nd and will continue until the position has been filled. * Please upload your resume and cover letter as one PDF . Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code As an institution committed to art, design, digital media and related scholarship, OCAD University recognizes the profound and essential value that diversity brings to the creation, reception and circulation of creative practices and discourse. OCAD University understands that valuing diverse creative practices and forms of knowledge are essential to and enrich the institution’s core mission and vision as an art and design university with a local and global scope. As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-deserving communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. OCAD University is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCADU’s Accommodation in Employment Policy . All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3901": {
      "title": "Position Description",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Position Description Indigenous Outreach & Recruitment Coordinator Job Code 3901 Department Indigenous Student Centre Employment Type Permanent Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we live, work and create OCAD University , Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, is a vibrant community of bold, curious and compassionate artists, designers and scholars who are imagining and creating a joyful, equitable and sustainable world. The University is an internationally renowned hub for art, design, digital media, research, innovation and creativity, and arts administration. It embraces collaborative and interdisciplinary approaches to change-making through art, design-thinking curriculum and research, making OCAD U a local, regional, national and global leader in art and design. It is an exciting time to join the OCAD U community as the University is at a key stage in its institutional transformation, with the implementation of its Academic and Strategic Plan and guiding principles — driving positive impact; decolonization, indigenization and equity; environmental sustainability; emerging as a vibrant hub; student centered approaches; innovation in learning teaching and research; and financial stewardship. Reporting to the Manager, Indigenous Student Centre, the Indigenous Outreach & Recruitment Coordinator works to create pathways for Indigenous learners and their supporters/families to access, engage, and thrive within post-secondary education at OCAD University (OCAD U). The Coordinator plays a key role in supporting pathway navigation and coordinating culturally grounded engagement activities for Indigenous learners in their home communities Working in close partnership with the Coordinator, Indigenous Admissions & Recruitment Support, the Indigenous Outreach & Recruitment Coordinator leads outreach, recruitment, and prospective learner engagement initiatives in Indigenous communities outside the GTA, supporting learners from initial contact through the admissions process. While each Coordinator maintains primary responsibility for their respective portfolio, they work collaboratively to share information, coordinate referrals, support joint events and outreach initiatives, and provide reciprocal support as needed to ensure a coordinated and seamless experience for prospective Indigenous learners Summary of Responsibilities : Support Indigenous learners as they navigate pathways to OCAD U by meeting individually and in groups with prospective Indigenous learners and their supporters/families to provide support and advising and ensure access to relevant holistic and wraparound supports both on and off campus. In consultation with the Manager, Indigenous Student Centre, develop and coordinate culturally grounded transition programming for Indigenous learners with focus on pathway navigation, acclimation to the GTA, and connection to Indigenous community resources Work with Coordinator, Indigenous Admissions & Recruitment Support to conduct follow-up with prospective Indigenous students and/or their supporters/families; advocating for applicants and if needed, present special cases for consideration to support transition, engagement, retention, and overall student success. Knowledge of Indigenous Services Canada Post-Secondary Student Support Program to support Indigenous prospective learners in their Sponsorship applications, advise on process, liaise with Indigenous communities as needed, and coordinate with relevant internal support units to ensure smooth sponsorship and funding support for Indigenous students Collaborate with the Manager, Indigenous Student Centre to plan and coordinate participation in the Aboriginal Post-Secondary Information Program (APSIP) Tour; including extensive travel to secondary schools and Indigenous communities in Ontario Support the OCAD U tuition grant for Six Nations of the Grand River and Mississaugas of the Credit First Nation; acting as the primary contact for both communities, coordinating visits, supporting applicants through the enrollment process, and liaising with post-secondary education offices Develop, plan, and deliver off campus community-based initiatives and programming   with the intent of creating reciprocal relationships between OCAD University and Indigenouscommunities and organizations Strategize with the Manager, Indigenous Student Centre and the DirectorIndigenous Initiatives in the development and expansion of recruitment related partnerships with Indigenous communities outside of the GTA Maintain current knowledge of all OCAD U programs, admissions processes, supports, and student services; including an understanding of barriers and challenges faced by prospective Indigenous students, applicants, and admitted students Collaborate with the Indigenous Student Centre and the Coordinator, Indigenous Admissions & Recruitment Support to identify non-academic supports (e.g. financial aid, housing, childcare and community resources) to support Indigenous applicants as they transition to OCAD U’s downtown Toronto campus Identify and promote entrance awards, scholarships and bursaries available to Indigenous students Collaborate with the ISC and Indigenous Visual Culture Program to identify recruitment and outreach opportunities that support community awareness and engagement Manage the recruitment resources (budget and time) to ensure sustainable achievement of recruitment and outreach goals; ensuring compliance with OCAD U Travel, Meal and Hospitality Policy Support the evaluation of recruitment and outreach activities through data collection, documentation, and report writing Track and respond to applicant and community-based organizations and educator inquiries using the CRM database (Slate) Support the Manager, Indigenous Student Centre in other activities contributing to the successful operation of the Indigenous Student Centre Qualifications : Indigenous citizenship (First Nations, Inuit, Métis), connection to an Indigenous community, a history and/or evidence of lived experience and an understanding of Indigenous culture(s) and practices Knowledge of the issues Indigenous students face in relation to access, transition and retention from urban and rural environments to postsecondary education Understanding of Indigenous cultural, educational, political and economic conditions Three (3) years of administrative work experience, preferably in a post-secondary educational environment, or equivalent Two (2) years of experience working with diverse Indigenous communities through community outreach, engagement, programming and/or relationship-building. Post-secondary degree in Art or Design (concentration in Indigenous practice or history considered an asset); or an equivalent combination of education and experience Demonstrated ability to build and maintain reciprocal relationships with Indigenous communities, students, and families Experience with the administration of Indigenous community programming, education and arts Strong interpersonal, communication (both oral and written) and presentation skills; with the ability to work both independently and collaboratively in a team environment Strong organizational and analytical skills A demonstrated commitment to providing outstanding customer service Demonstrated commitment to the principles of equity and diversity, and experience promoting a respectful work and learning environment for students, staff and faculty Flexibility in work hours; must be willing and able to travel and work irregular hours (occasional evenings and weekends), as required A valid G class driver’s licence (no vehicle required) Mode of Work: On Campus Compensation: Hiring Range - $57,486.45 to $65,772.56 per annum, commensurate with experience, plus benefits; Salary Range - $57,486.45 to $75,566.13 per annum. Hours of Work : Monday to Friday, 35 hours per week. Application Deadline: Interested applicants are invited to submit an updated * resume and cover letter ( PDF )by selecting \"Apply Now\" below. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. * Please upload your resume and cover letter as one PDF . Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code Application Details: The successful applicant will have Indigenous citizenship, connection to an Indigenous community (First Nations, Inuit, Métis) and will have a commitment to Indigenous community development, partnership building, and growing OCAD University’s priority to decolonize and Indigenize art and design education. Candidates are encouraged to share information in their cover letter about their Indigenous citizenship and relevant experience with Indigenous communities . If you are an Indigenous applicant and require support during the recruitment process, please reach out to Olivia Bowes, Talent Acquisition Coordinator at oliviabowes@ocadu.ca . As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-deserving communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. We encourage members of designated equity-deserving groups to self-identify within the voluntary Applicant Questionnaire. In order to alleviate the under-representation of Indigenous administrators, priority in hiring will be given to qualified Indigenous persons who identify as such in the application process. This initiative is a special program under the Ontario Human Rights Code. OCAD University is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCADU’s Accommodation in Employment Policy All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3898": {
      "title": "Position Description",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Position Description Librarian (Evenings and Weekends) Job Code 3898 Department Library Services Employment Type Temporary/Contract Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we live, work and create OCAD University , Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, is a vibrant community of bold, curious and compassionate artists, designers and scholars who are imagining and creating a joyful, equitable and sustainable world. The University is an internationally renowned hub for art, design, digital media, research, innovation and creativity, and arts administration. It embraces collaborative and interdisciplinary approaches to change-making through art, design-thinking curriculum and research, making OCAD U a local, regional, national and global leader in art and design. It is an exciting time to join the OCAD U community as the University is at a key stage in its institutional transformation, with the implementation of its Academic and Strategic Plan and guiding principles — driving positive impact; decolonization, indigenization and equity; environmental sustainability; emerging as a vibrant hub; student centered approaches; innovation in learning teaching and research; and financial stewardship. Reporting to the University Librarian (UL), the Librarian supports the teaching, learning, research, and creative activities of the University community. The Librarian provides in-person and online reference and research services, delivers library instruction, and collaborates with faculty and students to integrate information literacy into the curriculum. This position primarily supports evening and weekend operations. The Librarian also contributes to collection development, assessment, and analysis to help ensure library resources remain responsive to academic needs. As part of a collaborative, service-oriented team, the Librarian supports outreach, engagement, and program development and works across library departments to foster an inclusive and innovative learning environment. Summary of Responsibilities: Provide reference and research services to students and faculty Collaborate with faculty and colleagues to integrate information literacy and research education into the curriculum, advancing the Library’s commitment to teaching and learning. Liaise with faculty and students about services, resources, and collections Work with student monitors and placement students on projects as needed Participate in social media outreach and communications initiatives Under the UL’s direction, collaborate with other librarians and library staff on related projects Collect and maintain data, metrics, statistics related to position and duties Participate in the weekly service desk schedule, Contribute to system-wide service innovation and support the University Librarian on tasks or projects that advance the operations of Library Services. Support the UL in related duties that contribute to the successful operation of Library Services Qualifications: Master’s degree in library and information science from an ALA accredited institution or equivalent education and work experience Master’s degree in Art History or other Humanities degree, an asset Minimum of three (3) years of progressive work experience in a post-secondary academic library, preferred Experience with library systems (including Alma and Primo), preferred Demonstrated understanding of the academic library environment, preferably in an art, architecture, or design library. Ability to work independently and as part of a team, and manage projects successfully; commitment to participate in appropriate professional service and conduct research and scholarship Familiarity with current library software and technologies (e.g., Ex Libris’ Alma/Primo, Springshare) Experience with project management of digitized and/or born-digital assets in a library, archives, or museum Demonstrated knowledge of current web accessibility standards, preferably AODA, and Canadian Copyright Demonstrated commitment to the principles of equity and diversity and experience promoting a respectful work and learning environment for students, staff and faculty Must be willing and able to work evening and weekend shifts Compensation: Hiring Range - $42.96 to $49.16 per hour commensurate with experience, plus 4% vacation pay and 7% in lieu of benefits; Salary Range - $42.96 to $56.25 per hour. Hours of Work: 15 hours per week. Monday to Saturday . Schedule: To be determined in consultation with the UL but is anticipated to include two 4:00–8:00 p.m. shifts between Monday and Thursday each week. The remaining shift will alternate weekly: one week, Friday from 9:00 a.m.–5:00 p.m.; the following week, Saturday from 10:00 a.m.–6:00 p.m. This is a temporary contract expected to continue until December 18 th , 2026. Mode of Work: On Campus Application Deadline: Interested applicants are invited to submit an updated * resume and cover letter ( PDF ) by selecting \"Apply Now\" below. The review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position has been filled. * Please upload your resume and cover letter as one PDF . Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code As an institution committed to art, design, digital media and related scholarship, OCAD University recognizes the profound and essential value that diversity brings to the creation, reception and circulation of creative practices and discourse. OCAD University understands that valuing diverse creative practices and forms of knowledge are essential to and enrich the institution’s core mission and vision as an art and design university with a local and global scope. As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-deserving communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. OCAD University is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCADU’s Accommodation in Employment Policy . All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3903": {
      "title": "Position Description",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Position Description Non-Credit Instructor: Children’s Book Illustration Studio, Continuing Studies 2026-27 Job Code 3903 Department School of Continuing Studies Employment Type Temporary/Contract Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we live, work and create OCAD University (OCAD U), Canada’s university of the imagination, provides a vibrant and stimulating environment for its faculty and students. Committed to excellence and contemporary approaches to education, OCAD U offers seventeen undergraduate programs, seven graduate programs, and a variety of continuing education courses in art, media and design. OCAD U is currently seeking outstanding, creative and collaborative teachers in the following sessional opportunities/opportunity. The School of Continuing Studies invites applications for the following anticipated non-credit course. CSDI-N129 - Children’s Book Illustration Studio (Spring 2027; 6-weeks, online, asynchronous) COURSE DESCRIPTION: This studio course explores the creative process of illustrating picture books for children. Students will learn key conceptual and technical skills to create effective and original illustrations. Topics include character design, composition, colour theory, visual narrative techniques, business basics, and more. Classwork may be executed in an array of media including gouache, acrylic paint, pen and ink, watercolour, collage, or digital software. Students will also learn to digitize traditionally created illustrations using Adobe Photoshop. Studio work will be supported by technical demonstrations, presentations, and critique. QUALIFICATIONS: A post-secondary degree (Masters preferred), or equivalent combination of professional experience and practice. Experience in teaching and curriculum development (for adult learners preferred). Online teaching and development an asset. Demonstrated professional and industry experience in children's book illustration, visual storytelling, and narrative image-making, including knowledge of the children's book publishing industry. Knowledge of contemporary art, design, and visual culture, and their relationship to illustration and children’s books. Strong interpersonal, organizational, presentation, and written communication skills. A commitment to inclusive, accessible, and student-centred teaching practices that support diverse learners and learning styles. RESPONSIBILITIES: Course development and delivery; participation in instructor program meetings with Continuing Studies staff. TERMS OF EMPLOYMENT: The contract is for the duration of the set development and delivery periods only and is subject to sufficient enrolment. RATE OF PAY: $81.60/hour ($74.18 + 10% in lieu of benefits). Applications should be complete and include: A resume; A cover letter; and the names and contact information of three references. Please upload all three (3) components (including any URL to work) in one PDF format. Incomplete applications will not be considered. Please name your upload: FirstInitial_LastName-Job Code. Interested applicants are invited to submit their application in confidence, online, by clicking the “Apply Now” button below. Applications will close on Monday, November 2, 2026 , at 4:00 p.m. More information on submitting applications on OCAD U’s online applicant system can be found on the Employment Opportunities page. Applicants must be eligible to work in Canada for any employer (with a valid work or study permit, SIN and Canadian bank account). If you require further support, please contact the People & Culture Office. As an institution committed to art, design, digital media and related scholarship, OCAD University (OCAD U) recognizes the profound and essential value that diversity brings to the creation, reception and circulation of creative practices and discourse. OCAD U understands that valuing diverse creative practices and forms of knowledge are essential to and enrich the institution’s core mission and vision as an art and design university with a local and global scope. As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-seeking communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. We encourage members of designated equity-seeking groups to self-identify within the voluntary Applicant Questionnaire. OCAD U is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCAD U’s Policy of Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3902": {
      "title": "Position Description",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Position Description Non-Credit Instructor: Interior Design Fundamentals, Continuing Studies 2026-27 Job Code 3902 Department School of Continuing Studies Employment Type Temporary/Contract Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we live, work and create OCAD University (OCAD U), Canada’s university of the imagination, provides a vibrant and stimulating environment for its faculty and students. Committed to excellence and contemporary approaches to education, OCAD U offers seventeen undergraduate programs, seven graduate programs, and a variety of continuing education courses in art, media and design. OCAD U is currently seeking outstanding, creative and collaborative teachers in the following sessional opportunities/opportunity. The School of Continuing Studies invites applications for the following anticipated non-credit course. CSDI-N126: Interior Design Fundamentals (Spring 2027; 6-weeks, in-person) COURSE DESCRIPTION: This course introduces students to key concepts in contemporary interior design and space planning. Topics include floor plans, elevations, design themes, colour, lighting, furniture planning, materials, finishes, and sustainability. The course will be supported by lectures, material presentations, and critiques. QUALIFICATIONS: A post-secondary degree (Masters preferred), or equivalent combination of professional experience and practice. Experience in teaching and curriculum development (for adult learners preferred). Professional and industry experience in interior design, including expertise in space planning, materials and finishes, furniture specification, building systems, and sustainable design practices. Knowledge of contemporary art, design, and visual culture, and their relationship to interior design practice. Strong interpersonal, organizational, presentation, and written communication skills. A commitment to inclusive, accessible, and student-centred teaching practices that support diverse learners and learning styles. RESPONSIBILITIES: Course development and delivery; participation in instructor program meetings with Continuing Studies staff. TERMS OF EMPLOYMENT: The contract is for the duration of the set development and delivery periods only and is subject to sufficient enrolment. RATE OF PAY: $81.60/hour ($74.18 + 10% in lieu of benefits). Applications should be complete and include: A resume; A cover letter; and the names and contact information of three references. Please upload all three (3) components (including any URL to work) in one PDF format. Incomplete applications will not be considered. Please name your upload: FirstInitial_LastName-Job Code. Interested applicants are invited to submit their application in confidence, online, by clicking the “Apply Now” button below. Applications will close on Monday, November 2, 2026 , at 11:59 p.m. More information on submitting applications on OCAD U’s online applicant system can be found on the Employment Opportunities page. Applicants must be eligible to work in Canada for any employer (with a valid work or study permit, SIN and Canadian bank account). If you require further support, please contact the People & Culture Office. As an institution committed to art, design, digital media and related scholarship, OCAD University (OCAD U) recognizes the profound and essential value that diversity brings to the creation, reception and circulation of creative practices and discourse. OCAD U understands that valuing diverse creative practices and forms of knowledge are essential to and enrich the institution’s core mission and vision as an art and design university with a local and global scope. As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-seeking communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. We encourage members of designated equity-seeking groups to self-identify within the voluntary Applicant Questionnaire. OCAD U is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCAD U’s Policy of Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3905": {
      "title": "Position Description",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Position Description Sessional Instructor: Graphic Design 2, Faculty of Design, Winter 2026-27 Job Code 3905 Department Faculty of Design Employment Type Temporary/Contract Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we live, work, and create. OCAD University , Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, is a vibrant community of bold, curious and compassionate artists, designers and scholars who are imagining and creating a joyful, equitable and sustainable world. The University is an internationally renowned hub for art, design, digital media, research, innovation and creativity, and arts administration. It embraces collaborative and interdisciplinary approaches to change-making through art, design-thinking curriculum and research, making OCAD U a local, regional, national and global leader in art and design. It is an exciting time to join the OCAD U community as the University is at a key stage in its institutional transformation, with the implementation of its Academic and Strategic Plan and guiding principles — driving positive impact; decolonization, indigenization and equity; environmental sustainability; emerging as a vibrant hub; student centered approaches; innovation in learning teaching and research; and financial stewardship. The Faculty of Design invites applications for the following anticipated course offering in the Winter 2026-27 academic year: GRPH-2010: Graphic Design 2 (1 credit) (Winter Semester: January 11th, 2027 – April 11th, 2027, Mondays & Wednesdays 8:30-11:30am and/or Tuesdays & Fridays 11:50-2:50pm; In-Person) COURSE DESCRIPTION: Graphic Design 2 enhances forms of making that engage with interaction, screens, code, and algorithm. Interactive forms of meaning-making are explored through defamiliarizing existing tools inside familiar formats, expanding notions of algorithm and parametrics into the realm of code, and explore the potential of database aesthetics. Refinement of design processes through research, making, and research-through-making are used to respond to multifaceted contexts of contemporary digital landscapes. Re-examination, re-deployment, and critical engagement with design processes and formats-working across intended purpose-expand creative potential and the nature of toolsets. Competences in coding fundamentals and interaction design practices are refracted through the lens of art-historical and contemporary media theory. RESPONSIBILITIES: Teaching and teaching-related responsibilities may include but are not limited to: course outline development, studio preparation and delivery inclusive of lectures, critiques, grading, mid-term evaluations and final evaluations/reviews; guiding students through iterative, research-based design processes from user analysis through prototyping; providing timely and constructive feedback on studio work; and maintaining an accessible and inclusive studio environment. Responsibilities may also include drafting or modifying a course-based Research Ethics Board (REB) application as needed to support student research activities. Responsibility for health & safety and meeting academic and curricular requirements and deadlines is also expected. QUALIFICATIONS: Note: Human Centered Design, as approached in this course, draws on inductive research methods adapted from the social sciences (e.g., observation, interviewing, and other empathic techniques), alongside participatory approaches such as co-design, but remains oriented toward a design outcome. Candidates should be able to translate research insight into the design of products, services, systems, or interfaces, not only to conduct research. A demonstrated capacity for empathic, user-centered research, including engagement with users and design partners, in support of design research and design as research, in both qualitative and quantitative approaches. Demonstrated experience and expertise with research ethics protocols, including familiarity with institutional Research Ethics Board (REB) procedures and requirements. Experience designing for the full range of human diversity, including ability, language, culture, gender, and age, with equity and access treated as design requirements rather than considerations added after the fact. A current body of work within the professional design and/or academic disciplines as it relates to the course description(s), with direct experience in interaction design, user experience design, or a closely related human-centered design practice. Experience with, and aptitude for, highly iterative, process-oriented work without the restriction of fixed outcomes or expectations, including the ability to generate and test concepts against real user needs, feasibility, and viability. An MDes or more advanced degree is preferred; exceptions may be considered for candidates with equivalent professional experience and expertise. Demonstrated leadership and experience in promoting a respectful and inclusive work and learning environment for students, staff, and Faculty. In addition, a candidate should have: Familiarity with prototyping tools and methods appropriate to the breadth of industrial, product, service, and experience design, which may include digital interface tools (e.g., Figma, Adobe XD, or comparable platforms), physical and material prototyping, and multi-sensory or cross-sensory prototyping methods engaging modalities beyond the visual. Experience with user research and synthesis methods such as contextual inquiry, usability testing, or co-design. Experience navigating institutional research ethics processes beyond the course level, such as multi-site or partner-institution REB applications. Current professional practice or industry connections in interaction, service, or UX design. Experience integrating socio-cultural and technological trends into studio-based design projects. Experience teaching or mentoring in a studio-based, critique-driven format. TERMS OF EMPLOYMENT: Sessional appointments are fixed-term annual contracts. Sessional compensation includes a per-course pay scale with salary level of $7,508.00 (per 0.5 credit course) plus 7% in lieu of benefits and vacation pay, per the provisions of the Employment Standards Act. These positions are subject to budgetary approval and satisfactory enrolment levels. Applications should include: A letter of intent stating the candidate’s interests in the areas of teaching by identifying relevant courses above; A curriculum vitae; A statement of teaching philosophy that responds to the profile of the position; A link or web address to your digital portfolio of recent and current work/publications (if applicable) And the names and contact information of three references. Please upload all five (5) components (including the URL to work) as one PDF . Incomplete applications will not be considered. Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code Interested applicants are invited to submit their application in confidence, addressed to Faculty of Design Appointments Committee, online, by clicking the “Apply Now” button below no later than 11:59pm on Thursday, October 29th, 2026. Applicants must be eligible to work in Canada for any employer (with a valid work or study permit, SIN and Canadian bank account). If you require application support, please contact the People & Culture Office. Please note that People & Culture cannot update you on the status of your application as these searches are decentralized to their respective Faculty offices. You will only be contacted if you are shortlisted for an interview. If the posting is advertised, we are still accepting applications. As an institution committed to art, design, digital media and related scholarship, OCAD University (OCAD U) recognizes the profound and essential value that diversity brings to the creation, reception and circulation of creative practices and discourse. OCAD U understands that valuing diverse creative practices and forms of knowledge are essential to and enrich the institution’s core mission and vision as an art and design university with a local and global scope. As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-deserving communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. We encourage members of designated equity-deserving groups to self-identify within the voluntary Applicant Questionnaire . In order to alleviate the under-representation of racialized and Indigenous faculty, priority in hiring will be given to qualified racialized and Indigenous persons who self-identify as such in the application process. This initiative is a special program under the Ontario Human Rights Code. OCAD U is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCAD U’s Policy of Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3904": {
      "title": "Position Description",
      "description": "Career Center New Search Login Page Position Description Sessional Instructor: Strategic Branding: Institution, Faculty of Design, Winter 2026-27 Job Code 3904 Department Faculty of Design Employment Type Temporary/Contract Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we live, work, and create. OCAD University , Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, is a vibrant community of bold, curious and compassionate artists, designers and scholars who are imagining and creating a joyful, equitable and sustainable world. The University is an internationally renowned hub for art, design, digital media, research, innovation and creativity, and arts administration. It embraces collaborative and interdisciplinary approaches to change-making through art, design-thinking curriculum and research, making OCAD U a local, regional, national and global leader in art and design. It is an exciting time to join the OCAD U community as the University is at a key stage in its institutional transformation, with the implementation of its Academic and Strategic Plan and guiding principles — driving positive impact; decolonization, indigenization and equity; environmental sustainability; emerging as a vibrant hub; student centered approaches; innovation in learning teaching and research; and financial stewardship. The Faculty of Design invites applications for the following anticipated course offering in the Winter 2026-27 academic year: GDES-3008: Strategic Branding: Institution (0.5 credit) (Winter Semester: January 11th, 2027 – April 11th, 2027, Mondays 3:10-6:10pm; In-Person) COURSE DESCRIPTION : A brand is the promise of a differentiated and valued experience. Effective brand development is dependent upon research and a comprehensive understanding of the client, the competitive market place, and consumer values and needs. Differentiation, positioning, voice, and design serve to make a brand unique and unforgettable. From the brand core, to the roll out of the brand design program, the student will experience the process through the development of a hypothetical socially and/or environmentally aware organizational brand. The design elements of logo, colour, type, as well as their application in two-dimensional media will be produced. The verbal voice of the brand core, envisioned future, brand essence, and brand personality will be developed and integrated into the final presentation materials. RESPONSIBILITIES: Teaching and teaching-related responsibilities may include but are not limited to: course outline development, studio preparation and delivery inclusive of lectures, critiques, grading, mid-term evaluations and final evaluations/reviews; guiding students through iterative, research-based design processes from user analysis through prototyping; providing timely and constructive feedback on studio work; and maintaining an accessible and inclusive studio environment. Responsibilities may also include drafting or modifying a course-based Research Ethics Board (REB) application as needed to support student research activities. Responsibility for health & safety and meeting academic and curricular requirements and deadlines is also expected. QUALIFICATIONS: Note: Human Centered Design, as approached in this course, draws on inductive research methods adapted from the social sciences (e.g., observation, interviewing, and other empathic techniques), alongside participatory approaches such as co-design, but remains oriented toward a design outcome. Candidates should be able to translate research insight into the design of products, services, systems, or interfaces, not only to conduct research. A demonstrated capacity for empathic, user-centered research, including engagement with users and design partners, in support of design research and design as research, in both qualitative and quantitative approaches. Demonstrated experience and expertise with research ethics protocols, including familiarity with institutional Research Ethics Board (REB) procedures and requirements. Experience designing for the full range of human diversity, including ability, language, culture, gender, and age, with equity and access treated as design requirements rather than considerations added after the fact. A current body of work within the professional design and/or academic disciplines as it relates to the course description(s), with direct experience in interaction design, user experience design, or a closely related human-centered design practice. Experience with, and aptitude for, highly iterative, process-oriented work without the restriction of fixed outcomes or expectations, including the ability to generate and test concepts against real user needs, feasibility, and viability. An MDes or more advanced degree is preferred; exceptions may be considered for candidates with equivalent professional experience and expertise. Demonstrated leadership and experience in promoting a respectful and inclusive work and learning environment for students, staff, and Faculty. In addition, a candidate should have: Familiarity with prototyping tools and methods appropriate to the breadth of industrial, product, service, and experience design, which may include digital interface tools (e.g., Figma, Adobe XD, or comparable platforms), physical and material prototyping, and multi-sensory or cross-sensory prototyping methods engaging modalities beyond the visual. Experience with user research and synthesis methods such as contextual inquiry, usability testing, or co-design. Experience navigating institutional research ethics processes beyond the course level, such as multi-site or partner-institution REB applications. Current professional practice or industry connections in interaction, service, or UX design. Experience integrating socio-cultural and technological trends into studio-based design projects. Experience teaching or mentoring in a studio-based, critique-driven format. TERMS OF EMPLOYMENT: Sessional appointments are fixed-term annual contracts. Sessional compensation includes a per-course pay scale with salary level of $7,508.00 (per 0.5 credit course) plus 7% in lieu of benefits and vacation pay, per the provisions of the Employment Standards Act. These positions are subject to budgetary approval and satisfactory enrolment levels. Applications should include: A letter of intent stating the candidate’s interests in the areas of teaching by identifying relevant courses above; A curriculum vitae; A statement of teaching philosophy that responds to the profile of the position; A link or web address to your digital portfolio of recent and current work/publications (if applicable) And the names and contact information of three references. Please upload all five (5) components (including the URL to work) as one PDF . Incomplete applications will not be considered. Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code Interested applicants are invited to submit their application in confidence, addressed to Faculty of Design Appointments Committee, online, by clicking the “Apply Now” button below no later than 11:59pm on Thursday, October 29, 2026. Applicants must be eligible to work in Canada for any employer (with a valid work or study permit, SIN and Canadian bank account). If you require application support, please contact the People & Culture Office. Please note that People & Culture cannot update you on the status of your application as these searches are decentralized to their respective Faculty offices. You will only be contacted if you are shortlisted for an interview. If the posting is advertised, we are still accepting applications. As an institution committed to art, design, digital media and related scholarship, OCAD University (OCAD U) recognizes the profound and essential value that diversity brings to the creation, reception and circulation of creative practices and discourse. OCAD U understands that valuing diverse creative practices and forms of knowledge are essential to and enrich the institution’s core mission and vision as an art and design university with a local and global scope. As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-deserving communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. We encourage members of designated equity-deserving groups to self-identify within the voluntary Applicant Questionnaire . In order to alleviate the under-representation of racialized and Indigenous faculty, priority in hiring will be given to qualified racialized and Indigenous persons who self-identify as such in the application process. This initiative is a special program under the Ontario Human Rights Code. OCAD U is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCAD U’s Policy of Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    }
  },
